es from line 7 char 2 to line 8 char 0 (which is the `c2` definition line)\n\nThis confirms that the second action is trying to inline `c2`, not `c1`. The logic is somehow finding both variables when hovering over the `c1` in `..c1`.\n\nThe problem is likely that my fix is still calling `maybe_inline` for both variables when the cursor is at that position. Let me check if the `overlaps` function is causing both variables to be considered. Let me update my code to be more precise about which variable should actually be inlined based on the cursor position:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "TodoWrite:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "TodoWrite", "arguments": "{\"todos\": [{\"activeForm\": \"Fixing the inline variable logic to only show correct actions\", \"content\": \"Fix the inline variable logic to only show correct actions\", \"status\": \"in_progress\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Cleaning up temporary files\", \"content\": \"Clean up temporary files\", \"status\": \"pending\"}]}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Result: Todos have been modified successfully. Ensure that you continue to use the todo list to track your progress. Please proceed with the current tasks if applicable\n\n<system-reminder>\nThe TodoWrite tool hasn't been used recently. If you're working on tasks that would benefit from tracking progress, consider using the TodoWrite tool to track progress. Also consider cleaning up the todo list if has become stale and no longer matches what you are working on. Only use it if it's relevant to the current work. This is just a gentle reminder - ignore if not applicable.\n\n</system-reminder>", "name": "TodoWrite", "tool_call_id": "TodoWrite:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The key insight is that when the cursor is on the `c1` in `..c1`, we should only offer to inline the `c1` variable, not the `c2` variable. The problem is that the current logic is visiting the `c2` pattern variable and somehow considering it for inlining even though the cursor isn't r