in tests), but there is a reference to uint16_t in integration tests. This suggests that char16_t might not be handled as a distinct type currently.\n\nLet me look at:\n1. The known types file to see what primitive types are known\n2. The type conversion files\n3. The integration test to see if there's a test for char16_t\n\nLet me start with the known_types.rs and types.rs files."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "     1→// Copyright 2020 Google LLC\n     2→//\n     3→// Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 <LICENSE-APACHE or\n     4→// https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0> or the MIT license\n     5→// <LICENSE-MIT or https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT>, at your\n     6→// option. This file may not be copied, modified, or distributed\n     7→// except according to those terms.\n     8→\n     9→use crate::{\n    10→    conversion::ConvertError,\n    11→    types::{make_ident, QualifiedName},\n    12→};\n    13→use indoc::indoc;\n    14→use once_cell::sync::OnceCell;\n    15→use std::collections::HashMap;\n    16→use syn::{parse_quote, Type, TypePath, TypePtr};\n    17→\n    18→//// The behavior of the type.\n    19→#[derive(Debug)]\n    20→enum Behavior {\n    21→    CxxContainerByValueSafe,\n    22→    CxxContainerNotByValueSafe,\n    23→    CxxString,\n    24→    RustStr,\n    25→    RustString,\n    26→    RustByValue,\n    27→    CByValue,\n    28→    CVariableLengthByValue,\n    29→    CVoid,\n    30→    RustContainerByValueSafe,\n    31→}\n    32→\n    33→/// Details about known special types, mostly primitives.\n    34→#[derive(Debug)]\n    35→struct TypeDetails {\n    36→    /// The name used by cxx (in Rust code) for this type.\n    37→    rs_name: String,\n    38→    /// C++ equivalent name for a Rust type.\n    39→    cpp_name: String,\n    40→    /// The behavior of the type.\n    41→    behavior: Behavior,\n    42→    /// Any extra non-canonical names\n    43→    extra_non_canonical_name: Option<String>,\n    44→    has_const_copy_constructor: bool,\n    45→    has_move_constructor: bool,\n    46→}\n    47→\n    48→impl TypeDetails {\n    49→    fn new(\n    50→        rs_name: impl Into<String>,\n    51→        cpp_name: impl Into<String>,\n    52→        behavior: Behavior,\n    53→        extra_non_canonical_name: Option<String>,\n    54→        has_const_copy_constructor: bool,\n    55→        has_move_constructor: bool,\n    56→    ) -> Self {\n    57→        TypeDetails {\n    58→            rs_name: rs_name.into(),\n    59→            cpp_name: cpp_name.into(),\n    60→            behavior,\n    61→            extra_non_canonical_name,\n    62→            has_const_copy_constructor,\n    63→            has_move_constructor,\n    64→        }\n    65→    }\n    66→\n    67→    /// Whether and how to include this in the prelude given to bindgen.\n    68→    fn get_prelude_entry(&self) -> Option<String> {\n    69→        match self.behavior {\n    70→            Behavior::RustString\n    71→            | Behavior::RustStr\n    72→            | Behavior::CxxString\n    73→            | Behavior::CxxContainerByValueSafe\n    74→            | Behavior::CxxContainerNotByValueSafe\n    75→            | Behavior::RustContainerByValueSafe => {\n    76→                let tn = QualifiedName::new_from_cpp_name(&self.rs_name);\n    77→                let cxx_name = tn.get_final_item();\n    78→                let (templating, payload) = match self.behavior {\n    79→                    Behavior::CxxContainerByValueSafe\n    80→                    | Behavior::CxxContainerNotByValueSafe\n    81→                    | Behavior::RustContainerByValueSafe => (\"template<typename T> \", \"T* ptr\"),\n    82→                    _ => (\"\", \"char* ptr\"),\n    83→                };\n    84→                Some(format!(\n    85→                    indoc! {\"\n    86→                    /**\n    87→                    * <div rustbindgen=\\\"true\\\" replaces=\\\"{}\\\">\n    88→                    */\n    89→                    {}class {} {{\n    90→                        {};\n    91→                    }};\n    92→                    \"},\n    93→                    self.cpp_name, templating, cxx_name, payload\n    94→                ))\n    95→            }\n    96→            _ => None,\n    97→        }\n    98→    }\n    99→\n   100→    fn to_type_path(&self) -> TypePath {\n   101→        let mut segs = self.rs_name.split(\"::\").peekable();\n   102→        if segs.peek().map(|seg| seg.is_empty()).unwrap_or_default() {\n   103→            segs.next();\n   104→            let segs = segs.into_iter().map(make_ident);\n   105→            parse_quote! {\n   106→                ::#(#segs)::*\n   107→            }\n   108→        } else {\n   109→            let segs = segs.into_iter().map(make_ident);\n   110→            parse_quote! {\n   111→                #(#segs)::*\n   112→            }\n   113→        }\n   114→    }\n   115→\n   116→    fn to_typename(&self) -> QualifiedName {\n   117→        QualifiedName::new_from_cpp_name(&self.rs_name)\n   118→    }\n   119→\n   120→    fn get_generic_behavior(&self) -> CxxGenericType {\n   121→        match self.behavior {\n   122→            Behavior::CxxContainerByValueSafe | Behavior::CxxContainerNotByValueSafe => {\n   123→                CxxGenericType::Cpp\n   124→            }\n   125→            Behavior::RustContainerByValueSafe => CxxGenericType::Rust,\n   126→            _ => CxxGenericType::Not,\n   127→        }\n   128→    }\n   129→}\n   130→\n   131→/// Database of known types.\n   132→#[derive(Default)]\n   133→pub(crate) struct TypeDatabase {\n   134→    by_rs_name: HashMap<QualifiedName, TypeDetails>,\n   135→    canonical_names: HashMap<QualifiedName, QualifiedName>,\n   136→}\n   137→\n   138→/// Returns a database of known types.\n   139→pub(crate) fn known_types() -> &'static TypeDatabase {\n   140→    static KNOWN_TYPES: OnceCell<TypeDatabase> = OnceCell::new();\n   141→    KNOWN_TYPES.get_or_init(create_type_database)\n   142→}\n   143→\n   144→/// The type of payload that a cxx generic can contain.\n   145→#[derive(PartialEq, Clone, Copy)]\n   146→pub enum CxxGenericType {\n   147→    /// Not a generic at all\n   148→    Not,\n   149→    /// Some generic like cxx::UniquePtr where the contents must be a\n   150→    /// complete type.\n   151→    Cpp,\n   152→    /// Some generic like rust::Box where forward declarations are OK\n   153→    Rust,\n   154→}\n   155→\n   156→pub struct KnownTypeConstructorDetails {\n   157→    pub has_move_constructor: bool,\n   158→    pub has_const_copy_constructor: bool,\n   159→}\n   160→\n   161→impl TypeDatabase {\n   162→    fn get(&self, ty: &QualifiedName) -> Option<&TypeDetails> {\n   163→        // The following line is important. It says that\n   164→        // when we encounter something like 'std::unique_ptr'\n   165→        // in the bindgen-generated bindings, we'll immediately\n   166→        // start to refer to that as 'UniquePtr' henceforth.\n   167→        let canonical_name = self.canonical_names.get(ty).unwrap_or(ty);\n   168→        self.by_rs_name.get(canonical_name)\n   169→    }\n   170→\n   171→    /// Prelude of C++ for squirting into bindgen. This configures\n   172→    /// bindgen to output simpler types to replace some STL types\n   173→    /// that bindgen just can't cope with. Although we then replace\n   174→    /// those types with cxx types (e.g. UniquePtr), this intermediate\n   175→    /// step is still necessary because bindgen can't otherwise\n   176→    /// give us the templated types (e.g. when faced with the STL\n   177→    /// unique_ptr, bindgen would normally give us std_unique_ptr\n   178→    /// as opposed to std_unique_ptr<T>.)\n   179→    pub(crate) fn get_prelude(&self) -> String {\n   180→        itertools::join(\n   181→            self.by_rs_name\n   182→                .values()\n   183→                .filter_map(|t| t.get_prelude_entry()),\n   184→            \"\",\n   185→        )\n   186→    }\n   187→\n   188→    /// Returns all known types.\n   189→    pub(crate) fn all_names(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = &QualifiedName> {\n   190→        self.canonical_names.keys().chain(self.by_rs_name.keys())\n   191→    }\n   192→\n   193→    /// Types which are known to be safe (or unsafe) to hold and pass by\n   194→    /// value in Rust.\n   195→    pub(crate) fn get_pod_safe_types(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = (QualifiedName, bool)> {\n   196→        let pod_safety = self\n   197→            .all_names()\n   198→            .map(|tn| {\n   199→                (\n   200→                    tn.clone(),\n   201→                    match self.get(tn).unwrap().behavior {\n   202→                        Behavior::CxxContainerByValueSafe\n   203→                        | Behavior::RustStr\n   204→                        | Behavior::RustString\n   205→                        | Behavior::RustByValue\n   206→                        | Behavior::CByValue\n   207→                        | Behavior::CVariableLengthByValue\n   208→                        | Behavior::RustContainerByValueSafe => true,\n   209→                        Behavior::CxxString\n   210→                        | Behavior::CxxContainerNotByValueSafe\n   211→                        | Behavior::CVoid => false,\n   212→                    },\n   213→                )\n   214→            })\n   215→            .collect::<HashMap<_, _>>();\n   216→        pod_safety.into_iter()\n   217→    }\n   218→\n   219→    pub(crate) fn get_constructor_details(\n   220→        &self,\n   221→        qn: &QualifiedName,\n   222→    ) -> Option<KnownTypeConstructorDetails> {\n   223→        self.get(qn).map(|x| KnownTypeConstructorDetails {\n   224→            has_move_constructor: x.has_move_constructor,\n   225→            has_const_copy_constructor: x.has_const_copy_constructor,\n   226→        })\n   227→    }\n   228→\n   229→    /// Whether this TypePath should be treated as a value in C++\n   230→    /// but a reference in Rust. This only applies to rust::Str\n   231→    /// (C++ name) which is &str in Rust.\n   232→    pub(crate) fn should_dereference_in_cpp(&self, tn: &QualifiedName) -> bool {\n   233→        self.get(tn)\n   234→            .map(|td| matches!(td.behavior, Behavior::RustStr))\n   235→            .unwrap_or(false)\n   236→    }\n   237→\n   238→    /// Whether this can only be passed around using `std::move`\n   239→    pub(crate) fn lacks_copy_constructor(&self, tn: &QualifiedName) -> bool {\n   240→        self.get(tn)\n   241→            .map(|td| {\n   242→                matches!(\n   243→                    td.behavior,\n   244→                    Behavior::CxxContainerByValueSafe | Behavior::CxxContainerNotByValueSafe\n   245→                )\n   246→            })\n   247→            .unwrap_or(false)\n   248→    }\n   249→\n   250→    /// Here we substitute any names which we know are Special from\n   251→    /// our type database, e.g. std::unique_ptr -> UniquePtr.\n   252→    /// We strip off and ignore\n   253→    /// any PathArguments within this TypePath - callers should\n   254→    /// put them back again if needs be.\n   255→    pub(crate) fn consider_substitution(&self, tn: &QualifiedName) -> Option<TypePath> {\n   256→        self.get(tn).map(|td| td.to_type_path())\n   257→    }\n   258→\n   259→    pub(crate) fn special_cpp_name(&self, rs: &QualifiedName) -> Option<String> {\n   260→        self.get(rs).map(|x| x.cpp_name.to_string())\n   261→    }\n   262→\n   263→    pub(crate) fn is_known_type(&self, ty: &QualifiedName) -> bool {\n   264→        self.get(ty).is_some()\n   265→    }\n   266→\n   267→    pub(crate) fn known_type_type_path(&self, ty: &QualifiedName) -> Option<TypePath> {\n   268→        self.get(ty).map(|td| td.to_type_path())\n   269→    }\n   270→\n   271→    /// Get the list of types to give to bindgen to ask it _not_ to\n   272→    /// generate code for.\n   273→    pub(crate) fn get_initial_blocklist(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = &str> + '_ {\n   274→        self.by_rs_name\n   275→            .iter()\n   276→            .filter_map(|(_, td)| td.get_prelude_entry().map(|_| td.cpp_name.as_str()))\n   277→    }\n   278→\n   279→    /// Whether this is one of the ctypes (mostly variable length integers)\n   280→    /// which we need to wrap.\n   281→    pub(crate) fn is_ctype(&self, ty: &QualifiedName) -> bool {\n   282→        self.get(ty)\n   283→            .map(|td| {\n   284→                matches!(\n   285→                    td.behavior,\n   286→                    Behavior::CVariableLengthByValue | Behavior::CVoid\n   287→                )\n   288→            })\n   289→            .unwrap_or(false)\n   290→    }\n   291→\n   292→    /// Whether this is a generic type acceptable to cxx. Otherwise,\n   293→    /// if we encounter a generic, we'll replace it with a synthesized concrete\n   294→    /// type.\n   295→    pub(crate) fn cxx_generic_behavior(&self, ty: &QualifiedName) -> CxxGenericType {\n   296→        self.get(ty)\n   297→            .map(|x| x.get_generic_behavior())\n   298→            .unwrap_or(CxxGenericType::Not)\n   299→    }\n   300→\n   301→    pub(crate) fn is_cxx_acceptable_receiver(&self, ty: &QualifiedName) -> bool {\n   302→        self.get(ty).is_none() // at present, none of our known types can have\n   303→                               // methods attached.\n   304→    }\n   305→\n   306→    pub(crate) fn conflicts_with_built_in_type(&self, ty: &QualifiedName) -> bool {\n   307→        self.get(ty).is_some()\n   308→    }\n   309→\n   310→    pub(crate) fn convertible_from_strs(&self, ty: &QualifiedName) -> bool {\n   311→        self.get(ty)\n   312→            .map(|x| matches!(x.behavior, Behavior::CxxString))\n   313→            .unwrap_or(false)\n   314→    }\n   315→\n   316→    fn insert(&mut self, td: TypeDetails) {\n   317→        let rs_name = td.to_typename();\n   318→        if let Some(extra_non_canonical_name) = &td.extra_non_canonical_name {\n   319→            self.canonical_names.insert(\n   320→                QualifiedName::new_from_cpp_name(extra_non_canonical_name),\n   321→                rs_name.clone(),\n   322→            );\n   323→        }\n   324→        self.canonical_names.insert(\n   325→            QualifiedName::new_from_cpp_name(&td.cpp_name),\n   326→            rs_name.clone(),\n   327→        );\n   328→        self.by_rs_name.insert(rs_name, td);\n   329→    }\n   330→}\n   331→\n   332→fn create_type_database() -> TypeDatabase {\n   333→    let mut db = TypeDatabase::default();\n   334→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   335→        \"cxx::UniquePtr\",\n   336→        \"std::unique_ptr\",\n   337→        Behavior::CxxContainerByValueSafe,\n   338→        None,\n   339→        false,\n   340→        true,\n   341→    ));\n   342→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   343→        \"cxx::CxxVector\",\n   344→        \"std::vector\",\n   345→        Behavior::CxxContainerNotByValueSafe,\n   346→        None,\n   347→        false,\n   348→        true,\n   349→    ));\n   350→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   351→        \"cxx::SharedPtr\",\n   352→        \"std::shared_ptr\",\n   353→        Behavior::CxxContainerByValueSafe,\n   354→        None,\n   355→        true,\n   356→        true,\n   357→    ));\n   358→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   359→        \"cxx::WeakPtr\",\n   360→        \"std::weak_ptr\",\n   361→        Behavior::CxxContainerByValueSafe,\n   362→        None,\n   363→        true,\n   364→        true,\n   365→    ));\n   366→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   367→        \"cxx::CxxString\",\n   368→        \"std::string\",\n   369→        Behavior::CxxString,\n   370→        None,\n   371→        true,\n   372→        true,\n   373→    ));\n   374→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   375→        \"str\",\n   376→        \"rust::Str\",\n   377→        Behavior::RustStr,\n   378→        None,\n   379→        true,\n   380→        false,\n   381→    ));\n   382→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   383→        \"String\",\n   384→        \"rust::String\",\n   385→        Behavior::RustString,\n   386→        None,\n   387→        true,\n   388→        true,\n   389→    ));\n   390→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   391→        \"std::boxed::Box\",\n   392→        \"rust::Box\",\n   393→        Behavior::RustContainerByValueSafe,\n   394→        None,\n   395→        false,\n   396→        true,\n   397→    ));\n   398→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   399→        \"i8\",\n   400→        \"int8_t\",\n   401→        Behavior::CByValue,\n   402→        Some(\"std::os::raw::c_schar\".into()),\n   403→        true,\n   404→        true,\n   405→    ));\n   406→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   407→        \"u8\",\n   408→        \"uint8_t\",\n   409→        Behavior::CByValue,\n   410→        Some(\"std::os::raw::c_uchar\".into()),\n   411→        true,\n   412→        true,\n   413→    ));\n   414→    for (cpp_type, rust_type) in (4..7).map(|x| 2i32.pow(x)).flat_map(|x| {\n   415→        vec![\n   416→            (format!(\"uint{}_t\", x), format!(\"u{}\", x)),\n   417→            (format!(\"int{}_t\", x), format!(\"i{}\", x)),\n   418→        ]\n   419→    }) {\n   420→        db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   421→            rust_type,\n   422→            cpp_type,\n   423→            Behavior::CByValue,\n   424→            None,\n   425→            true,\n   426→            true,\n   427→        ));\n   428→    }\n   429→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   430→        \"bool\",\n   431→        \"bool\",\n   432→        Behavior::CByValue,\n   433→        None,\n   434→        true,\n   435→        true,\n   436→    ));\n   437→\n   438→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   439→        \"std::pin::Pin\",\n   440→        \"Pin\",\n   441→        Behavior::RustByValue, // because this is actually Pin<&something>\n   442→        None,\n   443→        true,\n   444→        false,\n   445→    ));\n   446→\n   447→    let mut insert_ctype = |cname: &str| {\n   448→        let concatenated_name = cname.replace(' ', \"\");\n   449→        db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   450→            format!(\"autocxx::c_{}\", concatenated_name),\n   451→            cname,\n   452→            Behavior::CVariableLengthByValue,\n   453→            Some(format!(\"std::os::raw::c_{}\", concatenated_name)),\n   454→            true,\n   455→            true,\n   456→        ));\n   457→        db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   458→            format!(\"autocxx::c_u{}\", concatenated_name),\n   459→            format!(\"unsigned {}\", cname),\n   460→            Behavior::CVariableLengthByValue,\n   461→            Some(format!(\"std::os::raw::c_u{}\", concatenated_name)),\n   462→            true,\n   463→            true,\n   464→        ));\n   465→    };\n   466→\n   467→    insert_ctype(\"long\");\n   468→    insert_ctype(\"int\");\n   469→    insert_ctype(\"short\");\n   470→    insert_ctype(\"long long\");\n   471→\n   472→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   473→        \"f32\",\n   474→        \"float\",\n   475→        Behavior::CByValue,\n   476→        None,\n   477→        true,\n   478→        true,\n   479→    ));\n   480→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   481→        \"f64\",\n   482→        \"double\",\n   483→        Behavior::CByValue,\n   484→        None,\n   485→        true,\n   486→        true,\n   487→    ));\n   488→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   489→        \"::std::os::raw::c_char\",\n   490→        \"char\",\n   491→        Behavior::CByValue,\n   492→        None,\n   493→        true,\n   494→        true,\n   495→    ));\n   496→    db.insert(TypeDetails::new(\n   497→        \"autocxx::c_void\",\n   498→        \"void\",\n   499→        Behavior::CVoid,\n   500→        Some(\"std::os::raw::c_void\".into()),\n   501→        false,\n   502→        false,\n   503→    ));\n   504→    db\n   505→}\n   506→\n   507→pub(crate) fn ensure_pointee_is_valid(ptr: &TypePtr) -> Result<(), ConvertError> {\n   508→    match *ptr.elem {\n   509→        Type::Path(..) => Ok(()),\n   510→        _ => Err(ConvertError::InvalidPointee),\n   511→    }\n   512→}\n   513→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:0"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"content_result\": \"contentResult=     1→// Copyright 2020 Google LLC\\n     2→//\\n     3→// Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 <LICENSE-APACHE or\\n     4→// https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0> or the MIT license\\n     5→// <LICENSE-MIT or https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT>, at your\\n     6→// option. This file may not be copied, modified, or distributed\\n     7→// except according to those terms.\\n     8→\\n     9→use itertools::Itertools;\\n    10→use proc_macro2::Span;\\n    11→use quote::ToTokens;\\n    12→use std::iter::Peekable;\\n    13→use std::{fmt::Display, sync::Arc};\\n    14→use syn::{parse_quote, Ident, PathSegment, TypePath};\\n    15→\\n    16→use crate::{conversion::ConvertError, known_types::known_types};\\n    17→\\n    18→pub(crate) fn make_ident<S: AsRef<str>>(id: S) -> Ident {\\n    19→    Ident::new(id.as_ref(), Span::call_site())\\n    20→}\\n    21→\\n    22→/// Newtype wrapper for a C++ namespace.\\n    23→#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, PartialOrd, Eq, Hash, Clone)]\\n    24→#[allow(clippy::rc_buffer)]\\n    25→pub struct Namespace(Arc<Vec<String>>);\\n    26→\\n    27→impl Namespace {\\n    28→    pub(crate) fn new() -> Self {\\n    29→        Self(Arc::new(Vec::new()))\\n    30→    }\\n    31→\\n    32→    #[must_use]\\n    33→    pub(crate) fn push(&self, segment: String) -> Self {\\n    34→        let mut bigger = (*self.0).clone();\\n    35→        bigger.push(segment);\\n    36→        Namespace(Arc::new(bigger))\\n    37→    }\\n    38→\\n    39→    pub(crate) fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {\\n    40→        self.0.is_empty()\\n    41→    }\\n    42→\\n    43→    pub(crate) fn iter(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = &String> {\\n    44→        self.0.iter()\\n    45→    }\\n    46→\\n    47→    #[cfg(test)]\\n    48→    pub(crate) fn from_user_input(input: &str) -> Self {\\n    49→        Self(Arc::new(input.split(\\\"::\\\").map(|x| x.to_string()).collect()))\\n    50→    }\\n    51→\\n    52→    pub(crate) fn depth(&self) -> usize {\\n    53→        self.0.len()\\n    54→    }\\n    55→\\n    56→    pub(crate) fn to_display_suffix(&self) -> String {\\n    57→        if self.is_empty() {\\n    58→            String::new()\\n    59→        } else {\\n    60→            format!(\\\" (in namespace {})\\\", self)\\n    61→        }\\n    62→    }\\n    63→}\\n    64→\\n    65→impl Display for Namespace {\\n    66→    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\\n    67→        f.write_str(&self.0.join(\\\"::\\\"))\\n    68→    }\\n    69→}\\n    70→\\n    71→impl<'a> IntoIterator for &'a Namespace {\\n    72→    type Item = &'a String;\\n    73→\\n    74→    type IntoIter = std::slice::Iter<'a, String>;\\n    75→\\n    76→    fn into_iter(self) -> Self::IntoIter {\\n    77→        self.0.iter()\\n    78→    }\\n    79→}\\n    80→\\n    81→/// Any time we store a qualified name, we should use this. Stores the type\\n    82→/// and its namespace. Namespaces should be stored without any\\n    83→/// 'bindgen::root' prefix; that means a type not in any C++\\n    84→/// namespace should have an empty namespace segment list.\\n    85→/// Some types have names that change as they flow through the\\n    86→/// autocxx pipeline. e.g. you start with std::string\\n    87→/// and end up with CxxString. This TypeName type can store\\n    88→/// either. It doesn't directly have functionality to convert\\n    89→/// from one to the other; `replace_type_path_without_arguments`\\n    90→/// does that.\\n    91→#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, PartialOrd, Eq, Hash, Clone)]\\n    92→pub struct QualifiedName(Namespace, String);\\n    93→\\n    94→impl QualifiedName {\\n    95→    /// From a TypePath which starts with 'root'\\n    96→    pub(crate) fn from_type_path(typ: &TypePath) -> Self {\\n    97→        let mut seg_iter = typ.path.segments.iter().peekable();\\n    98→        let first_seg = seg_iter.next().unwrap().ident.clone();\\n    99→        if first_seg == \\\"root\\\" {\\n   100→            // This is a C++ type prefixed with a namespace,\\n   101→            // e.g. std::string or something the user has defined.\\n   102→            Self::from_segments(seg_iter) // all but 'root'\\n   103→        } else {\\n   104→            // This is actually a Rust type e.g.\\n   105→            // std::os::raw::c_ulong. Start iterating from the beginning again.\\n   106→            Self::from_segments(typ.path.segments.iter().peekable())\\n   107→        }\\n   108→    }\\n   109→\\n   110→    fn from_segments<'a, T: Iterator<Item = &'a PathSegment>>(mut seg_iter: Peekable<T>) -> Self {\\n   111→        let mut ns = Namespace::new();\\n   112→        while let Some(seg) = seg_iter.next() {\\n   113→            if seg_iter.peek().is_some() {\\n   114→                ns = ns.push(seg.ident.to_string());\\n   115→            } else {\\n   116→                return Self(ns, seg.ident.to_string());\\n   117→            }\\n   118→        }\\n   119→        unreachable!()\\n   120→    }\\n   121→\\n   122→    /// Create from a type encountered in the code.\\n   123→    pub(crate) fn new(ns: &Namespace, id: Ident) -> Self {\\n   124→        Self(ns.clone(), id.to_string())\\n   125→    }\\n   126→\\n   127→    /// Create from user input, e.g. a name in an AllowPOD directive.\\n   128→    pub(crate) fn new_from_cpp_name(id: &str) -> Self {\\n   129→        let mut seg_iter = id.split(\\\"::\\\").peekable();\\n   130→        let mut ns = Namespace::new();\\n   131→        while let Some(seg) = seg_iter.next() {\\n   132→            if seg_iter.peek().is_some() {\\n   133→                if !seg.to_string().is_empty() {\\n   134→                    ns = ns.push(seg.to_string());\\n   135→                }\\n   136→            } else {\\n   137→                return Self(ns, seg.to_string());\\n   138→            }\\n   139→        }\\n   140→        unreachable!()\\n   141→    }\\n   142→\\n   143→    /// Return the actual type name, without any namespace\\n   144→    /// qualification. Avoid unless you have a good reason.\\n   145→    pub(crate) fn get_final_item(&self) -> &str {\\n   146→        &self.1\\n   147→    }\\n   148→\\n   149→    /// cxx doesn't accept names containing double underscores,\\n   150→    /// but these are OK elsewhere in our output mod.\\n   151→    pub(crate) fn validate_ok_for_cxx(&self) -> Result<(), ConvertError> {\\n   152→        validate_ident_ok_for_cxx(self.get_final_item())\\n   153→    }\\n   154→\\n   155→    /// Return the actual type name as an [Ident], without any namespace\\n   156→    /// qualification. Avoid unless you have a good reason.\\n   157→    pub(crate) fn get_final_ident(&self) -> Ident {\\n   158→        make_ident(self.get_final_item())\\n   159→    }\\n   160→\\n   161→    pub(crate) fn get_namespace(&self) -> &Namespace {\\n   162→        &self.0\\n   163→    }\\n   164→\\n   165→    pub(crate) fn get_bindgen_path_idents(&self) -> Vec<Ident> {\\n   166→        [\\\"bindgen\\\", \\\"root\\\"]\\n   167→            .iter()\\n   168→            .map(make_ident)\\n   169→            .chain(self.ns_segment_iter().map(make_ident))\\n   170→            .chain(std::iter::once(self.get_final_ident()))\\n   171→            .collect()\\n   172→    }\\n   173→\\n   174→    /// Output the fully-qualified C++ name of this type.\\n   175→    pub(crate) fn to_cpp_name(&self) -> String {\\n   176→        let special_cpp_name = known_types().special_cpp_name(self);\\n   177→        match special_cpp_name {\\n   178→            Some(name) => name,\\n   179→            None => self.0.iter().chain(std::iter::once(&self.1)).join(\\\"::\\\"),\\n   180→        }\\n   181→    }\\n   182→\\n   183→    pub(crate) fn get_final_cpp_item(&self) -> String {\\n   184→        let special_cpp_name = known_types().special_cpp_name(self);\\n   185→        match special_cpp_name {\\n   186→            Some(name) => name,\\n   187→            None => self.1.to_string(),\\n   188→        }\\n   189→    }\\n   190→\\n   191→    pub(crate) fn to_type_path(&self) -> TypePath {\\n   192→        if let Some(known_type_path) = known_types().known_type_type_path(self) {\\n   193→            known_type_path\\n   194→        } else {\\n   195→            let root = \\\"root\\\".to_string();\\n   196→            let segs = std::iter::once(&root)\\n   197→                .chain(self.ns_segment_iter())\\n   198→                .chain(std::iter::once(&self.1))\\n   199→                .map(make_ident);\\n   200→            parse_quote! {\\n   201→                #(#segs)::*\\n   202→            }\\n   203→        }\\n   204→    }\\n   205→\\n   206→    /// Iterator over segments in the namespace of this name.\\n   207→    pub(crate) fn ns_segment_iter(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = &String> {\\n   208→        self.0.iter()\\n   209→    }\\n   210→\\n   211→    /// Iterate over all segments of this name.\\n   212→    pub(crate) fn segment_iter(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = String> + '_ {\\n   213→        self.ns_segment_iter()\\n   214→            .cloned()\\n   215→            .chain(std::iter::once(self.get_final_item().to_string()))\\n   216→    }\\n   217→}\\n   218→\\n   219→impl Display for QualifiedName {\\n   220→    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\\n   221→        for seg in &self.0 {\\n   222→            f.write_str(seg)?;\\n   223→            f.write_str(\\\"::\\\")?;\\n   224→        }\\n   225→        f.write_str(&self.1)\\n   226→    }\\n   227→}\\n   228→\\n   229→/// cxx doesn't allow identifiers containing __. These are OK elsewhere\\n   230→/// in our output mod. It would be nice in future to think of a way we\\n   231→/// can enforce this using the Rust type system, e.g. a newtype\\n   232→/// wrapper for a CxxCompatibleIdent which is used in any context\\n   233→/// where code will be output as part of the `#[cxx::bridge]` mod.\\n   234→pub fn validate_ident_ok_for_cxx(id: &str) -> Result<(), ConvertError> {\\n   235→    validate_ident_ok_for_rust(id)?;\\n   236→    if id.contains(\\\"__\\\") {\\n   237→        Err(ConvertError::TooManyUnderscores)\\n   238→    } else {\\n   239→        Ok(())\\n   240→    }\\n   241→}\\n   242→\\n   243→pub fn validate_ident_ok_for_rust(label: &str) -> Result<(), ConvertError> {\\n   244→    let id = make_ident(label);\\n   245→    syn::parse2::<syn::Ident>(id.into_token_stream())\\n   246→        .map_err(|_| ConvertError::ReservedName(label.to_string()))\\n   247→        .map(|_| ())\\n   248→}\\n   249→\\n   250→#[cfg(test)]\\n   251→mod tests {\\n   252→    use super::QualifiedName;\\n   253→\\n   254→    #[test]\\n   255→    fn test_ints() {\\n   256→        assert_eq!(\\n   257→            QualifiedName::new_from_cpp_name(\\\"i8\\\").to_cpp_name(),\\n   258→            \\\"int8_t\\\"\\n   259→        );\\n   260→        assert_eq!(\\n   261→            QualifiedName::new_from_cpp_name(\\\"u64\\\").to_cpp_name(),\\n   262→            \\\"uint64_t\\\"\\n   263→        );\\n   264→    }\\n   265→}\\n   266→\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:1"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "No matches found\n\n<system-reminder>\nThe TodoWrite tool hasn't been used recently. If you're working on tasks that would benefit from tracking progress, consider using the TodoWrite tool to track progress. Also consider cleaning up the todo list if has become stale and no longer matches what you are working on. Only use it if it's relevant to the current work. This is just a gentle reminder - ignore if not applicable.\n\n</system-reminder>", "name": "grep", "tool_call_id": "grep:2"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me search for test files that might contain char16_t tests:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Bash:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"find /workspace/autocxx -name \\\"*.rs\\\" -type f -exec grep -l \\\"char16\\\" {} \\\\;\", \"description\": \"Find all Rust files containing char16\"}"}}, {"id": "Bash:1", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"find /workspace/autocxx -name \\\"*.sh\\\" -type f\", \"description\": \"Find shell scripts\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "The integration test doesn't currently have any char16_t tests. Let me look for the actual test file that was mentioned in the issue to understand what test was created."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"TEXT_RESULT\": \"type = text\\ntext =\"}", "name": "Bash", "tool_call_id": "Bash:0"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "/workspace/autocxx/swe-gen-install.sh\n/workspace/autocxx/swe-gen-run-test.sh\n/workspace/autocxx/tools/upgrade-cxx.sh\n/workspace/autocxx/tools/upgrade-version.sh\n/workspace/autocxx/tools/publish-all.sh\n\n<system-reminder>\nThe TodoWrite tool hasn't been used recently. If you're working on tasks that would benefit from tracking progress, consider using the TodoWrite tool to track progress. Also consider cleaning up the todo list if has become stale and no longer matches what you are working on. Only use it if it's relevant to the current work. This is just a gentle reminder - ignore if not applicable.\n\n</system-reminder>", "name": "Bash", "tool_call_id": "Bash:1"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Read:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/autocxx/swe-gen-run-test.sh\"}"}}, {"id": "Read:1", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/autocxx/swe-gen-install.sh\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Let me check these shell scripts to understand what tests are being run and how to run them."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "     1→#!/bin/bash\n     2→set -e\n     3→\n     4→# Run the full test suite for the workspace, excluding mdbook-preprocessor due to compatibility issues\n     5→# Based on CI configuration, this runs all tests except on Windows where\n     6→# some packages are excluded due to creduce dependency\n     7→cargo test --workspace --exclude autocxx-mdbook-preprocessor\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:0"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"Text\": \"     1→#!/bin/bash\\n     2→set -e\\n     3→\\n     4→# Install Rust toolchain components needed for the project\\n     5→rustup component add rustfmt\\n     6→\\n     7→# Build all workspace dependencies to install/cache them, excluding mdbook-preprocessor due to compatibility issues\\n     8→cargo build --workspace --exclude autocxx-mdbook-preprocessor\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:1"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me explore how autocxx handles template types and concrete type generation:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "grep:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"OUTPUTMODE\": \"files_with_matches\", \"pattern\": \"Concrete|concrete\"}"}}, {"id": "grep:1", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"OUTPUTMODE\": \"files_with_matches\", \"pattern\": \"AutocxxConcrete\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Now let me understand the error message better. The error shows that:\n- autocxx generates code with `url_StringPieceReplacements_uint16_t_AutocxxConcrete`\n- But the C++ function expects either `StringPieceReplacements<char>` or `StringPieceReplacements<char16_t>`\n\nSo the issue is that when autocxx encounters a template instantiation like `StringPieceReplacements<char16_t>`, it's treating `char16_t` as `uint16_t` instead of keeping it as `char16_t`.\n\nLet me look at how autocxx generates concrete template type names. I should look for code that deals with template concretization."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Text-Result: 'Found 15 files\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/src/lib.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/parser/src/config.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/integration-tests/tests/integration_test.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/parse/parse_bindgen.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/engine/src/known_types.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/error_reporter.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/codegen_rs/mod.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/api.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/codegen_cpp/mod.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/analysis/pod/byvalue_checker.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/analysis/type_converter.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/analysis/fun/mod.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/analysis/name_check.rs\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/book/src/cpp_types.md\n\n  /workspace/autocxx/.git/packed-refs'", "name": "grep", "tool_call_id": "grep:0"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"content_result=Found 2 files\\n/workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/analysis/pod/byvalue_checker.rs\\n/workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/analysis/type_converter.rs\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nThe TodoWrite tool hasn't been used recently. If you're working on tasks that would benefit from tracking progress, consider using the TodoWrite tool to track progress. Also consider cleaning up the todo list if has become stale and no longer matches what you are working on. Only use it if it's relevant to the current work. This is just a gentle reminder - ignore if not applicable.\\n\\n</system-reminder>\"}", "name": "grep", "tool_call_id": "grep:1"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Read:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/analysis/type_converter.rs\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Great! Let me look at the type_converter.rs file since it likely handles the conversion of types including generating concrete type names."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"TEXT_RESULT\": \"     1→// Copyright 2020 Google LLC\\n     2→//\\n     3→// Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 <LICENSE-APACHE or\\n     4→// https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0> or the MIT license\\n     5→// <LICENSE-MIT or https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT>, at your\\n     6→// option. This file may not be copied, modified, or distributed\\n     7→// except according to those terms.\\n     8→\\n     9→use crate::{\\n    10→    conversion::{\\n    11→        api::{AnalysisPhase, Api, ApiName, NullPhase, TypedefKind, UnanalyzedApi},\\n    12→        apivec::ApiVec,\\n    13→        codegen_cpp::type_to_cpp::type_to_cpp,\\n    14→        ConvertError,\\n    15→    },\\n    16→    known_types::{known_types, CxxGenericType},\\n    17→    types::{make_ident, Namespace, QualifiedName},\\n    18→};\\n    19→use autocxx_parser::IncludeCppConfig;\\n    20→use itertools::Itertools;\\n    21→use proc_macro2::Ident;\\n    22→use quote::ToTokens;\\n    23→use std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};\\n    24→use syn::{\\n    25→    parse_quote, punctuated::Punctuated, GenericArgument, PathArguments, PathSegment, Type,\\n    26→    TypePath, TypePtr,\\n    27→};\\n    28→\\n    29→use super::tdef::TypedefAnalysis;\\n    30→\\n    31→/// Certain kinds of type may require special handling by callers.\\n    32→#[derive(Debug)]\\n    33→pub(crate) enum TypeKind {\\n    34→    Regular,\\n    35→    Pointer,\\n    36→    SubclassHolder(Ident),\\n    37→    Reference,\\n    38→    MutableReference,\\n    39→}\\n    40→\\n    41→/// Results of some type conversion, annotated with a list of every type encountered,\\n    42→/// and optionally any extra APIs we need in order to use this type.\\n    43→pub(crate) struct Annotated<T> {\\n    44→    pub(crate) ty: T,\\n    45→    pub(crate) types_encountered: HashSet<QualifiedName>,\\n    46→    pub(crate) extra_apis: ApiVec<NullPhase>,\\n    47→    pub(crate) kind: TypeKind,\\n    48→}\\n    49→\\n    50→impl<T> Annotated<T> {\\n    51→    fn new(\\n    52→        ty: T,\\n    53→        types_encountered: HashSet<QualifiedName>,\\n    54→        extra_apis: ApiVec<NullPhase>,\\n    55→        kind: TypeKind,\\n    56→    ) -> Self {\\n    57→        Self {\\n    58→            ty,\\n    59→            types_encountered,\\n    60→            extra_apis,\\n    61→            kind,\\n    62→        }\\n    63→    }\\n    64→\\n    65→    fn map<T2, F: FnOnce(T) -> T2>(self, fun: F) -> Annotated<T2> {\\n    66→        Annotated {\\n    67→            ty: fun(self.ty),\\n    68→            types_encountered: self.types_encountered,\\n    69→            extra_apis: self.extra_apis,\\n    70→            kind: self.kind,\\n    71→        }\\n    72→    }\\n    73→}\\n    74→\\n    75→/// Options when converting a type.\\n    76→/// It's possible we could add more policies here in future.\\n    77→/// For example, Rust in general allows type names containing\\n    78→/// __, whereas cxx doesn't. If we could identify cases where\\n    79→/// a type will only ever be used in a bindgen context,\\n    80→/// we could be more liberal. At the moment though, all outputs\\n    81→/// from [TypeConverter] _might_ be used in the [cxx::bridge].\\n    82→pub(crate) enum TypeConversionContext {\\n    83→    CxxInnerType,\\n    84→    CxxOuterType { convert_ptrs_to_references: bool },\\n    85→}\\n    86→\\n    87→impl TypeConversionContext {\\n    88→    fn convert_ptrs_to_references(&self) -> bool {\\n    89→        matches!(\\n    90→            self,\\n    91→            TypeConversionContext::CxxOuterType {\\n    92→                convert_ptrs_to_references: true,\\n    93→                ..\\n    94→            }\\n    95→        )\\n    96→    }\\n    97→    fn allow_instantiation_of_forward_declaration(&self) -> bool {\\n    98→        matches!(self, TypeConversionContext::CxxInnerType)\\n    99→    }\\n   100→}\\n   101→\\n   102→/// A type which can convert from a type encountered in `bindgen`\\n   103→/// output to the sort of type we should represeent to `cxx`.\\n   104→/// As a simple example, `std::string` should be replaced\\n   105→/// with [CxxString]. This also involves keeping track\\n   106→/// of typedefs, and any instantiated concrete types.\\n   107→///\\n   108→/// To do this conversion correctly, this type relies on\\n   109→/// inspecting the pre-existing list of APIs.\\n   110→pub(crate) struct TypeConverter<'a> {\\n   111→    types_found: HashSet<QualifiedName>,\\n   112→    typedefs: HashMap<QualifiedName, Type>,\\n   113→    concrete_templates: HashMap<String, QualifiedName>,\\n   114→    forward_declarations: HashSet<QualifiedName>,\\n   115→    config: &'a IncludeCppConfig,\\n   116→}\\n   117→\\n   118→impl<'a> TypeConverter<'a> {\\n   119→    pub(crate) fn new<A: AnalysisPhase>(config: &'a IncludeCppConfig, apis: &ApiVec<A>) -> Self\\n   120→    where\\n   121→        A::TypedefAnalysis: TypedefTarget,\\n   122→    {\\n   123→        Self {\\n   124→            types_found: find_types(apis),\\n   125→            typedefs: Self::find_typedefs(apis),\\n   126→            concrete_templates: Self::find_concrete_templates(apis),\\n   127→            forward_declarations: Self::find_incomplete_types(apis),\\n   128→            config,\\n   129→        }\\n   130→    }\\n   131→\\n   132→    pub(crate) fn convert_boxed_type(\\n   133→        &mut self,\\n   134→        ty: Box<Type>,\\n   135→        ns: &Namespace,\\n   136→        ctx: &TypeConversionContext,\\n   137→    ) -> Result<Annotated<Box<Type>>, ConvertError> {\\n   138→        Ok(self.convert_type(*ty, ns, ctx)?.map(Box::new))\\n   139→    }\\n   140→\\n   141→    pub(crate) fn convert_type(\\n   142→        &mut self,\\n   143→        ty: Type,\\n   144→        ns: &Namespace,\\n   145→        ctx: &TypeConversionContext,\\n   146→    ) -> Result<Annotated<Type>, ConvertError> {\\n   147→        let result = match ty {\\n   148→            Type::Path(p) => {\\n   149→                let newp = self.convert_type_path(p, ns, ctx)?;\\n   150→                if let Type::Path(newpp) = &newp.ty {\\n   151→                    let qn = QualifiedName::from_type_path(newpp);\\n   152→                    if !ctx.allow_instantiation_of_forward_declaration()\\n   153→                        && self.forward_declarations.contains(&qn)\\n   154→                    {\\n   155→                        return Err(ConvertError::TypeContainingForwardDeclaration(qn));\\n   156→                    }\\n   157→                    // Special handling because rust_Str (as emitted by bindgen)\\n   158→                    // doesn't simply get renamed to a different type _identifier_.\\n   159→                    // This plain type-by-value (as far as bindgen is concerned)\\n   160→                    // is actually a &str.\\n   161→                    if known_types().should_dereference_in_cpp(&qn) {\\n   162→                        Annotated::new(\\n   163→                            Type::Reference(parse_quote! {\\n   164→                                &str\\n   165→                            }),\\n   166→                            newp.types_encountered,\\n   167→                            newp.extra_apis,\\n   168→                            TypeKind::Reference,\\n   169→                        )\\n   170→                    } else {\\n   171→                        newp\\n   172→                    }\\n   173→                } else {\\n   174→                    newp\\n   175→                }\\n   176→            }\\n   177→            Type::Reference(mut r) => {\\n   178→                let innerty =\\n   179→                    self.convert_boxed_type(r.elem, ns, &TypeConversionContext::CxxInnerType)?;\\n   180→                r.elem = innerty.ty;\\n   181→                Annotated::new(\\n   182→                    Type::Reference(r),\\n   183→                    innerty.types_encountered,\\n   184→                    innerty.extra_apis,\\n   185→                    TypeKind::Reference,\\n   186→                )\\n   187→            }\\n   188→            Type::Ptr(ptr) if ctx.convert_ptrs_to_references() => {\\n   189→                self.convert_ptr_to_reference(ptr, ns)?\\n   190→            }\\n   191→            Type::Ptr(mut ptr) => {\\n   192→                crate::known_types::ensure_pointee_is_valid(&ptr)?;\\n   193→                let innerty =\\n   194→                    self.convert_boxed_type(ptr.elem, ns, &TypeConversionContext::CxxInnerType)?;\\n   195→                ptr.elem = innerty.ty;\\n   196→                Annotated::new(\\n   197→                    Type::Ptr(ptr),\\n   198→                    innerty.types_encountered,\\n   199→                    innerty.extra_apis,\\n   200→                    TypeKind::Pointer,\\n   201→                )\\n   202→            }\\n   203→            _ => return Err(ConvertError::UnknownType(ty.to_token_stream().to_string())),\\n   204→        };\\n   205→        Ok(result)\\n   206→    }\\n   207→\\n   208→    fn convert_type_path(\\n   209→        &mut self,\\n   210→        mut typ: TypePath,\\n   211→        ns: &Namespace,\\n   212→        ctx: &TypeConversionContext,\\n   213→    ) -> Result<Annotated<Type>, ConvertError> {\\n   214→        // First, qualify any unqualified paths.\\n   215→        if typ.path.segments.iter().next().unwrap().ident != \\\"root\\\" {\\n   216→            let ty = QualifiedName::from_type_path(&typ);\\n   217→            // If the type looks like it is unqualified, check we know it\\n   218→            // already, and if not, qualify it according to the current\\n   219→            // namespace. This is a bit of a shortcut compared to having a full\\n   220→            // resolution pass which can search all known namespaces.\\n   221→            if !known_types().is_known_type(&ty) {\\n   222→                let num_segments = typ.path.segments.len();\\n   223→                if num_segments > 1 {\\n   224→                    return Err(ConvertError::UnsupportedBuiltInType(ty));\\n   225→                }\\n   226→                if !self.types_found.contains(&ty) {\\n   227→                    typ.path.segments = std::iter::once(&\\\"root\\\".to_string())\\n   228→                        .chain(ns.iter())\\n   229→                        .map(|s| {\\n   230→                            let i = make_ident(s);\\n   231→                            parse_quote! { #i }\\n   232→                        })\\n   233→                        .chain(typ.path.segments.into_iter())\\n   234→                        .collect();\\n   235→                }\\n   236→            }\\n   237→        }\\n   238→\\n   239→        let original_tn = QualifiedName::from_type_path(&typ);\\n   240→        original_tn.validate_ok_for_cxx()?;\\n   241→        if self.config.is_on_blocklist(&original_tn.to_cpp_name()) {\\n   242→            return Err(ConvertError::Blocked(original_tn));\\n   243→        }\\n   244→        let mut deps = HashSet::new();\\n   245→\\n   246→        // Now convert this type itself.\\n   247→        deps.insert(original_tn.clone());\\n   248→        // First let's see if this is a typedef.\\n   249→        let (typ, tn) = match self.resolve_typedef(&original_tn)? {\\n   250→            None => (typ, original_tn),\\n   251→            Some(Type::Path(resolved_tp)) => {\\n   252→                let resolved_tn = QualifiedName::from_type_path(resolved_tp);\\n   253→                deps.insert(resolved_tn.clone());\\n   254→                (resolved_tp.clone(), resolved_tn)\\n   255→            }\\n   256→            Some(Type::Ptr(resolved_tp)) => {\\n   257→                return Ok(Annotated::new(\\n   258→                    Type::Ptr(resolved_tp.clone()),\\n   259→                    deps,\\n   260→                    ApiVec::new(),\\n   261→                    TypeKind::Pointer,\\n   262→                ))\\n   263→            }\\n   264→            Some(other) => {\\n   265→                return Ok(Annotated::new(\\n   266→                    other.clone(),\\n   267→                    deps,\\n   268→                    ApiVec::new(),\\n   269→                    TypeKind::Regular,\\n   270→                ))\\n   271→            }\\n   272→        };\\n   273→\\n   274→        // Now let's see if it's a known type.\\n   275→        // (We may entirely reject some types at this point too.)\\n   276→        let mut typ = match known_types().consider_substitution(&tn) {\\n   277→            Some(mut substitute_type) => {\\n   278→                if let Some(last_seg_args) =\\n   279→                    typ.path.segments.into_iter().last().map(|ps| ps.arguments)\\n   280→                {\\n   281→                    let last_seg = substitute_type.path.segments.last_mut().unwrap();\\n   282→                    last_seg.arguments = last_seg_args;\\n   283→                }\\n   284→                substitute_type\\n   285→            }\\n   286→            None => typ,\\n   287→        };\\n   288→\\n   289→        let mut extra_apis = ApiVec::new();\\n   290→        let mut kind = TypeKind::Regular;\\n   291→\\n   292→        // Finally let's see if it's generic.\\n   293→        if let Some(last_seg) = Self::get_generic_args(&mut typ) {\\n   294→            let generic_behavior = known_types().cxx_generic_behavior(&tn);\\n   295→            let forward_declarations_ok = generic_behavior == CxxGenericType::Rust\\n   296→                || ctx.allow_instantiation_of_forward_declaration();\\n   297→            if generic_behavior != CxxGenericType::Not {\\n   298→                // this is a type of generic understood by cxx (e.g. CxxVector)\\n   299→                // so let's convert any generic type arguments. This recurses.\\n   300→                kind = self.confirm_inner_type_is_acceptable_generic_payload(\\n   301→                    &last_seg.arguments,\\n   302→                    &tn,\\n   303→                    generic_behavior,\\n   304→                    forward_declarations_ok,\\n   305→                )?;\\n   306→                if let PathArguments::AngleBracketed(ref mut ab) = last_seg.arguments {\\n   307→                    let mut innerty = self.convert_punctuated(ab.args.clone(), ns)?;\\n   308→                    ab.args = innerty.ty;\\n   309→                    deps.extend(innerty.types_encountered.drain());\\n   310→                }\\n   311→            } else {\\n   312→                // Oh poop. It's a generic type which cxx won't be able to handle.\\n   313→                // We'll have to come up with a concrete type in both the cxx::bridge (in Rust)\\n   314→                // and a corresponding typedef in C++.\\n   315→                let (new_tn, api) = self.get_templated_typename(&Type::Path(typ))?;\\n   316→                extra_apis.extend(api.into_iter());\\n   317→                deps.remove(&tn);\\n   318→                typ = new_tn.to_type_path();\\n   319→                deps.insert(new_tn);\\n   320→            }\\n   321→        }\\n   322→        Ok(Annotated::new(Type::Path(typ), deps, extra_apis, kind))\\n   323→    }\\n   324→\\n   325→    fn get_generic_args(typ: &mut TypePath) -> Option<&mut PathSegment> {\\n   326→        match typ.path.segments.last_mut() {\\n   327→            Some(s) if !s.arguments.is_empty() => Some(s),\\n   328→            _ => None,\\n   329→        }\\n   330→    }\\n   331→\\n   332→    fn convert_punctuated<P>(\\n   333→        &mut self,\\n   334→        pun: Punctuated<GenericArgument, P>,\\n   335→        ns: &Namespace,\\n   336→    ) -> Result<Annotated<Punctuated<GenericArgument, P>>, ConvertError>\\n   337→    where\\n   338→        P: Default,\\n   339→    {\\n   340→        let mut new_pun = Punctuated::new();\\n   341→        let mut types_encountered = HashSet::new();\\n   342→        let mut extra_apis = ApiVec::new();\\n   343→        for arg in pun.into_iter() {\\n   344→            new_pun.push(match arg {\\n   345→                GenericArgument::Type(t) => {\\n   346→                    let mut innerty =\\n   347→                        self.convert_type(t, ns, &TypeConversionContext::CxxInnerType)?;\\n   348→                    types_encountered.extend(innerty.types_encountered.drain());\\n   349→                    extra_apis.append(&mut innerty.extra_apis);\\n   350→                    GenericArgument::Type(innerty.ty)\\n   351→                }\\n   352→                _ => arg,\\n   353→            });\\n   354→        }\\n   355→        Ok(Annotated::new(\\n   356→            new_pun,\\n   357→            types_encountered,\\n   358→            extra_apis,\\n   359→            TypeKind::Regular,\\n   360→        ))\\n   361→    }\\n   362→\\n   363→    fn resolve_typedef<'b>(&'b self, tn: &QualifiedName) -> Result<Option<&'b Type>, ConvertError> {\\n   364→        let mut encountered = HashSet::new();\\n   365→        let mut tn = tn.clone();\\n   366→        let mut previous_typ = None;\\n   367→        loop {\\n   368→            let r = self.typedefs.get(&tn);\\n   369→            match r {\\n   370→                Some(Type::Path(typ)) => {\\n   371→                    previous_typ = r;\\n   372→                    let new_tn = QualifiedName::from_type_path(typ);\\n   373→                    if encountered.contains(&new_tn) {\\n   374→                        return Err(ConvertError::InfinitelyRecursiveTypedef(tn.clone()));\\n   375→                    }\\n   376→                    encountered.insert(new_tn.clone());\\n   377→                    tn = new_tn;\\n   378→                }\\n   379→                None => return Ok(previous_typ),\\n   380→                _ => return Ok(r),\\n   381→            }\\n   382→        }\\n   383→    }\\n   384→\\n   385→    fn convert_ptr_to_reference(\\n   386→        &mut self,\\n   387→        ptr: TypePtr,\\n   388→        ns: &Namespace,\\n   389→    ) -> Result<Annotated<Type>, ConvertError> {\\n   390→        let mutability = ptr.mutability;\\n   391→        let elem = self.convert_boxed_type(ptr.elem, ns, &TypeConversionContext::CxxInnerType)?;\\n   392→        // TODO - in the future, we should check if this is a rust::Str and throw\\n   393→        // a wobbler if not. rust::Str should only be seen _by value_ in C++\\n   394→        // headers; it manifests as &str in Rust but on the C++ side it must\\n   395→        // be a plain value. We should detect and abort.\\n   396→        let mut outer = elem.map(|elem| match mutability {\\n   397→            Some(_) => Type::Path(parse_quote! {\\n   398→                ::std::pin::Pin < & #mutability #elem >\\n   399→            }),\\n   400→            None => Type::Reference(parse_quote! {\\n   401→                & #elem\\n   402→            }),\\n   403→        });\\n   404→        outer.kind = if mutability.is_some() {\\n   405→            TypeKind::MutableReference\\n   406→        } else {\\n   407→            TypeKind::Reference\\n   408→        };\\n   409→        Ok(outer)\\n   410→    }\\n   411→\\n   412→    fn get_templated_typename(\\n   413→        &mut self,\\n   414→        rs_definition: &Type,\\n   415→    ) -> Result<(QualifiedName, Option<UnanalyzedApi>), ConvertError> {\\n   416→        let count = self.concrete_templates.len();\\n   417→        // We just use this as a hash key, essentially.\\n   418→        // TODO: Once we've completed the TypeConverter refactoring (see #220),\\n   419→        // pass in an actual original_name_map here.\\n   420→        let cpp_definition = type_to_cpp(rs_definition, &HashMap::new())?;\\n   421→        let e = self.concrete_templates.get(&cpp_definition);\\n   422→        match e {\\n   423→            Some(tn) => Ok((tn.clone(), None)),\\n   424→            None => {\\n   425→                let synthetic_ident = format!(\\n   426→                    \\\"{}_AutocxxConcrete\\\",\\n   427→                    cpp_definition.replace(|c: char| !(c.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || c == '_'), \\\"_\\\")\\n   428→                );\\n   429→                // Remove runs of multiple _s. Trying to avoid a dependency on\\n   430→                // regex.\\n   431→                let synthetic_ident = synthetic_ident\\n   432→                    .split('_')\\n   433→                    .filter(|s| !s.is_empty())\\n   434→                    .join(\\\"_\\\");\\n   435→                // Ensure we're not duplicating some existing concrete template name.\\n   436→                // If so, we'll invent a name which is guaranteed to be unique.\\n   437→                let synthetic_ident = match self\\n   438→                    .concrete_templates\\n   439→                    .values()\\n   440→                    .map(|n| n.get_final_item())\\n   441→                    .find(|s| s == &synthetic_ident)\\n   442→                {\\n   443→                    None => synthetic_ident,\\n   444→                    Some(_) => format!(\\\"AutocxxConcrete{}\\\", count),\\n   445→                };\\n   446→                let api = UnanalyzedApi::ConcreteType {\\n   447→                    name: ApiName::new_in_root_namespace(make_ident(&synthetic_ident)),\\n   448→                    cpp_definition: cpp_definition.clone(),\\n   449→                    rs_definition: Some(Box::new(rs_definition.clone())),\\n   450→                };\\n   451→                self.concrete_templates\\n   452→                    .insert(cpp_definition, api.name().clone());\\n   453→                Ok((api.name().clone(), Some(api)))\\n   454→            }\\n   455→        }\\n   456→    }\\n   457→\\n   458→    fn confirm_inner_type_is_acceptable_generic_payload(\\n   459→        &self,\\n   460→        path_args: &PathArguments,\\n   461→        desc: &QualifiedName,\\n   462→        generic_behavior: CxxGenericType,\\n   463→        forward_declarations_ok: bool,\\n   464→    ) -> Result<TypeKind, ConvertError> {\\n   465→        // For now, all supported generics accept the same payloads. This\\n   466→        // may change in future in which case we'll need to accept more arguments here.\\n   467→        match path_args {\\n   468→            PathArguments::None => Ok(TypeKind::Regular),\\n   469→            PathArguments::Parenthesized(_) => Err(\\n   470→                ConvertError::TemplatedTypeContainingNonPathArg(desc.clone()),\\n   471→            ),\\n   472→            PathArguments::AngleBracketed(ab) => {\\n   473→                for inner in &ab.args {\\n   474→                    match inner {\\n   475→                        GenericArgument::Type(Type::Path(typ)) => {\\n   476→                            let inner_qn = QualifiedName::from_type_path(typ);\\n   477→                            if !forward_declarations_ok\\n   478→                                && self.forward_declarations.contains(&inner_qn)\\n   479→                            {\\n   480→                                return Err(ConvertError::TypeContainingForwardDeclaration(\\n   481→                                    inner_qn,\\n   482→                                ));\\n   483→                            }\\n   484→                            if generic_behavior == CxxGenericType::Rust {\\n   485→                                if !inner_qn.get_namespace().is_empty() {\\n   486→                                    return Err(ConvertError::RustTypeWithAPath(inner_qn));\\n   487→                                }\\n   488→                                if !self.config.is_rust_type(&inner_qn.get_final_ident()) {\\n   489→                                    return Err(ConvertError::BoxContainingNonRustType(inner_qn));\\n   490→                                }\\n   491→                                if self\\n   492→                                    .config\\n   493→                                    .is_subclass_holder(&inner_qn.get_final_ident().to_string())\\n   494→                                {\\n   495→                                    return Ok(TypeKind::SubclassHolder(\\n   496→                                        inner_qn.get_final_ident(),\\n   497→                                    ));\\n   498→                                } else {\\n   499→                                    return Ok(TypeKind::Regular);\\n   500→                                }\\n   501→                            }\\n   502→                            if let Some(more_generics) = typ.path.segments.last() {\\n   503→                                self.confirm_inner_type_is_acceptable_generic_payload(\\n   504→                                    &more_generics.arguments,\\n   505→                                    desc,\\n   506→                                    generic_behavior,\\n   507→                                    forward_declarations_ok,\\n   508→                                )?;\\n   509→                            }\\n   510→                        }\\n   511→                        _ => {\\n   512→                            return Err(ConvertError::TemplatedTypeContainingNonPathArg(\\n   513→                                desc.clone(),\\n   514→                            ))\\n   515→                        }\\n   516→                    }\\n   517→                }\\n   518→                Ok(TypeKind::Regular)\\n   519→            }\\n   520→        }\\n   521→    }\\n   522→\\n   523→    fn find_typedefs<A: AnalysisPhase>(apis: &ApiVec<A>) -> HashMap<QualifiedName, Type>\\n   524→    where\\n   525→        A::TypedefAnalysis: TypedefTarget,\\n   526→    {\\n   527→        apis.iter()\\n   528→            .filter_map(|api| match &api {\\n   529→                Api::Typedef { analysis, .. } => analysis\\n   530→                    .get_target()\\n   531→                    .cloned()\\n   532→                    .map(|ty| (api.name().clone(), ty)),\\n   533→                _ => None,\\n   534→            })\\n   535→            .collect()\\n   536→    }\\n   537→\\n   538→    fn find_concrete_templates<A: AnalysisPhase>(\\n   539→        apis: &ApiVec<A>,\\n   540→    ) -> HashMap<String, QualifiedName> {\\n   541→        apis.iter()\\n   542→            .filter_map(|api| match &api {\\n   543→                Api::ConcreteType { cpp_definition, .. } => {\\n   544→                    Some((cpp_definition.clone(), api.name().clone()))\\n   545→                }\\n   546→                _ => None,\\n   547→            })\\n   548→            .collect()\\n   549→    }\\n   550→\\n   551→    fn find_incomplete_types<A: AnalysisPhase>(apis: &ApiVec<A>) -> HashSet<QualifiedName> {\\n   552→        apis.iter()\\n   553→            .filter_map(|api| match api {\\n   554→                Api::ForwardDeclaration { .. } => Some(api.name()),\\n   555→                _ => None,\\n   556→            })\\n   557→            .cloned()\\n   558→            .collect()\\n   559→    }\\n   560→}\\n   561→\\n   562→/// Processing functions sometimes results in new types being materialized.\\n   563→/// These types haven't been through the analysis phases (chicken and egg\\n   564→/// problem) but fortunately, don't need to. We need to keep the type\\n   565→/// system happy by adding an [ApiAnalysis] but in practice, for the sorts\\n   566→/// of things that get created, it's always blank.\\n   567→pub(crate) fn add_analysis<A: AnalysisPhase>(api: UnanalyzedApi) -> Api<A> {\\n   568→    match api {\\n   569→        Api::ConcreteType {\\n   570→            name,\\n   571→            rs_definition,\\n   572→            cpp_definition,\\n   573→        } => Api::ConcreteType {\\n   574→            name,\\n   575→            rs_definition,\\n   576→            cpp_definition,\\n   577→        },\\n   578→        Api::IgnoredItem { name, err, ctx } => Api::IgnoredItem { name, err, ctx },\\n   579→        _ => panic!(\\\"Function analysis created an unexpected type of extra API\\\"),\\n   580→    }\\n   581→}\\n   582→pub(crate) trait TypedefTarget {\\n   583→    fn get_target(&self) -> Option<&Type>;\\n   584→}\\n   585→\\n   586→impl TypedefTarget for () {\\n   587→    fn get_target(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\\n   588→        None\\n   589→    }\\n   590→}\\n   591→\\n   592→impl TypedefTarget for TypedefAnalysis {\\n   593→    fn get_target(&self) -> Option<&Type> {\\n   594→        match self.kind {\\n   595→            TypedefKind::Type(ref ty) => Some(&ty.ty),\\n   596→            TypedefKind::Use(_) => None,\\n   597→        }\\n   598→    }\\n   599→}\\n   600→\\n   601→pub(crate) fn find_types<A: AnalysisPhase>(apis: &ApiVec<A>) -> HashSet<QualifiedName> {\\n   602→    apis.iter()\\n   603→        .filter_map(|api| match api {\\n   604→            Api::ForwardDeclaration { .. }\\n   605→            | Api::ConcreteType { .. }\\n   606→            | Api::Typedef { .. }\\n   607→            | Api::Enum { .. }\\n   608→            | Api::Struct { .. }\\n   609→            | Api::Subclass { .. }\\n   610→            | Api::RustType { .. } => Some(api.name()),\\n   611→            Api::StringConstructor { .. }\\n   612→            | Api::Function { .. }\\n   613→            | Api::Const { .. }\\n   614→            | Api::CType { .. }\\n   615→            | Api::RustSubclassFn { .. }\\n   616→            | Api::IgnoredItem { .. }\\n   617→            | Api::SubclassTraitItem { .. }\\n   618→            | Api::RustFn { .. } => None,\\n   619→        })\\n   620→        .cloned()\\n   621→        .collect()\\n   622→}\\n   623→\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Now let me check the `type_to_cpp` function which generates the C++ type names:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Read:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/codegen_cpp/type_to_cpp.rs\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Now I'm looking at the key part. In `get_templated_typename` function (lines 412-456), I can see where the concrete type name is generated:\n\n```rust\nlet cpp_definition = type_to_cpp(rs_definition, &HashMap::new())?;\n...\nlet synthetic_ident = format!(\n    \"{}_AutocxxConcrete\",\n    cpp_definition.replace(|c: char| !(c.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || c == '_'), \"_\")\n);\n```\n\nSo it's calling `type_to_cpp` to get the C++ definition. Let me look at that function since that's likely where the issue is - it's probably converting `char16_t` to `uint16_t`."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "1→// Copyright 2020 Google LLC\n     2→//\n     3→// Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 <LICENSE-APACHE or\n     4→// https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0> or the MIT license\n     5→// <LICENSE-MIT or https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT>, at your\n     6→// option. This file may not be copied, modified, or distributed\n     7→// except according to those terms.\n     8→\n     9→use crate::{\n    10→    conversion::{apivec::ApiVec, AnalysisPhase, ConvertError},\n    11→    types::QualifiedName,\n    12→};\n    13→use itertools::Itertools;\n    14→use quote::ToTokens;\n    15→use std::collections::HashMap;\n    16→use std::iter::once;\n    17→use syn::{Token, Type};\n    18→\n    19→/// Map from QualifiedName to original C++ name. Original C++ name does not\n    20→/// include the namespace; this can be assumed to be the same as the namespace\n    21→/// in the QualifiedName.\n    22→pub(crate) type CppNameMap = HashMap<QualifiedName, String>;\n    23→\n    24→pub(crate) fn original_name_map_from_apis<T: AnalysisPhase>(apis: &ApiVec<T>) -> CppNameMap {\n    25→    apis.iter()\n    26→        .filter_map(|api| {\n    27→            api.cpp_name()\n    28→                .as_ref()\n    29→                .map(|cpp_name| (api.name().clone(), cpp_name.clone()))\n    30→        })\n    31→        .collect()\n    32→}\n    33→\n    34→pub(crate) fn namespaced_name_using_original_name_map(\n    35→    qual_name: &QualifiedName,\n    36→    original_name_map: &CppNameMap,\n    37→) -> String {\n    38→    if let Some(cpp_name) = original_name_map.get(qual_name) {\n    39→        qual_name\n    40→            .get_namespace()\n    41→            .iter()\n    42→            .chain(once(cpp_name))\n    43→            .join(\"::\")\n    44→    } else {\n    45→        qual_name.to_cpp_name()\n    46→    }\n    47→}\n    48→\n    49→pub(crate) fn final_ident_using_original_name_map(\n    50→    qual_name: &QualifiedName,\n    51→    original_name_map: &CppNameMap,\n    52→) -> String {\n    53→    match original_name_map.get(qual_name) {\n    54→        Some(original_name) => {\n    55→            // If we have an original name, this may be a nested struct\n    56→            // (e.g. A::B). The final ident here is just 'B' so...\n    57→            original_name\n    58→                .rsplit_once(\"::\")\n    59→                .map_or(original_name.clone(), |(_, original_name)| {\n    60→                    original_name.to_string()\n    61→                })\n    62→        }\n    63→        None => qual_name.get_final_cpp_item(),\n    64→    }\n    65→}\n    66→\n    67→pub(crate) fn type_to_cpp(ty: &Type, cpp_name_map: &CppNameMap) -> Result<String, ConvertError> {\n    68→    match ty {\n    69→        Type::Path(typ) => {\n    70→            // If this is a std::unique_ptr we do need to pass\n    71→            // its argument through.\n    72→            let qual_name = QualifiedName::from_type_path(typ);\n    73→            let root = namespaced_name_using_original_name_map(&qual_name, cpp_name_map);\n    74→            if root == \"Pin\" {\n    75→                // Strip all Pins from type names when describing them in C++.\n    76→                let inner_type = &typ.path.segments.last().unwrap().arguments;\n    77→                if let syn::PathArguments::AngleBracketed(ab) = inner_type {\n    78→                    let inner_type = ab.args.iter().next().unwrap();\n    79→                    if let syn::GenericArgument::Type(gat) = inner_type {\n    80→                        return type_to_cpp(gat, cpp_name_map);\n    81→                    }\n    82→                }\n    83→                panic!(\"Pin<...> didn't contain the inner types we expected\");\n    84→            }\n    85→            let suffix = match &typ.path.segments.last().unwrap().arguments {\n    86→                syn::PathArguments::AngleBracketed(ab) => {\n    87→                    let results: Result<Vec<_>, _> = ab\n    88→                        .args\n    89→                        .iter()\n    90→                        .map(|x| match x {\n    91→                            syn::GenericArgument::Type(gat) => type_to_cpp(gat, cpp_name_map),\n    92→                            _ => Ok(\"\".to_string()),\n    93→                        })\n    94→                        .collect();\n    95→                    Some(results?.join(\", \"))\n    96→                }\n    97→                syn::PathArguments::None | syn::PathArguments::Parenthesized(_) => None,\n    98→            };\n    99→            match suffix {\n   100→                None => Ok(root),\n   101→                Some(suffix) => Ok(format!(\"{}<{}>\", root, suffix)),\n   102→            }\n   103→        }\n   104→        Type::Reference(typr) => Ok(format!(\n   105→            \"{}{}&\",\n   106→            get_mut_string(&typr.mutability),\n   107→            type_to_cpp(typr.elem.as_ref(), cpp_name_map)?\n   108→        )),\n   109→        Type::Ptr(typp) => Ok(format!(\n   110→            \"{}{}*\",\n   111→            get_mut_string(&typp.mutability),\n   112→            type_to_cpp(typp.elem.as_ref(), cpp_name_map)?\n   113→        )),\n   114→        Type::Array(_)\n   115→        | Type::BareFn(_)\n   116→        | Type::Group(_)\n   117→        | Type::ImplTrait(_)\n   118→        | Type::Infer(_)\n   119→        | Type::Macro(_)\n   120→        | Type::Never(_)\n   121→        | Type::Paren(_)\n   122→        | Type::Slice(_)\n   123→        | Type::TraitObject(_)\n   124→        | Type::Tuple(_)\n   125→        | Type::Verbatim(_) => Err(ConvertError::UnsupportedType(\n   126→            ty.to_token_stream().to_string(),\n   127→        )),\n   128→        _ => Err(ConvertError::UnknownType(ty.to_token_stream().to_string())),\n   129→    }\n   130→}\n   131→\n   132→fn get_mut_string(mutability: &Option<Token![mut]>) -> &'static str {\n   133→    match mutability {\n   134→        None => \"const \",\n   135→        Some(_) => \"\",\n   136→    }\n   137→}\n   138→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n\n<system-reminder>\nThe TodoWrite tool hasn't been used recently. If you're working on tasks that would benefit from tracking progress, consider using the TodoWrite tool to track progress. Also consider cleaning up the todo list if has become stale and no longer matches what you are working on. Only use it if it's relevant to the current work. This is just a gentle reminder - ignore if not applicable.\n\n</system-reminder>", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let me search for how bindgen processes types and look for any existing handling of char types:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "grep:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"OUTPUTMODE\": \"files_with_matches\", \"pattern\": \"bindgen\"}"}}, {"id": "Bash:1", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"rg \\\"c_char|wchar\\\" --type rust\", \"description\": \"Search for char type handling\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Now I need to understand how bindgen handles `char16_t`. Let me search for where bindgen's output is processed and how the types are recognized. The key issue is that bindgen is likely seeing `char16_t` and converting it to `u16` or `uint16_t`, and autocxx is accepting that conversion.\n\nLooking at the `to_cpp_name()` in `QualifiedName` (types.rs:175-181), it uses `known_types().special_cpp_name(self)`, and if that doesn't exist, it uses the namespace + name.\n\nSo if bindgen converts `char16_t` to `u16`, then when autocxx tries to map it back to C++, it will use the known_types database which maps `u16` to `uint16_t` (from known_types.rs:414-428).\n\nThe problem is:\n1. bindgen sees `char16_t` in C++ and converts it to `u16` in Rust (this is correct, since `char16_t` is typically 16-bit unsigned)\n2. autocxx then needs to convert this back to C++ for the concrete template name\n3. autocxx looks up `u16` in the known types and gets `uint16_t`\n4. But this is wrong! The original C++ type was `char16_t`, not `uint16_t`\n\nSo the fix needs to:\n1. Recognize when bindgen has converted a `char16_t` to `u16`\n2. Map it back to `char16_t` instead of `uint16_t` when generating C++ code\n\nBut wait, there's a complication. Both `char16_t` and `uint16_t` map to `u16` in Rust. How do we know which one was the original?\n\nLooking at the error message again:\n```\nurl_StringPieceReplacements_uint16_t_AutocxxConcrete\n```\n\nThis suggests that autocxx is generating a concrete type name based on the Rust type `u16`, which gets mapped to `uint16_t`. But the actual C++ function expects `char16_t`.\n\n\nI see a potential solution involving type preservation. The `CppNameMap` seems designed to retain original type information, but it appears to fall short with primitive types. \n\nI'll investigate bindgen's type processing mechanisms to understand how `char16_t` gets transformed. The goal is to find a way to track the original type through the conversion pipeline, ensuring we can distinguish between `char16_t` and `uint16_t` when they both map to `u16` in Rust.\n\nMy approach will focus on tracing how primitive types are handled during the translation from C++ to Rust, looking for opportunities to annotate or preserve the specific type origin."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "| Key | Value |\n|-----|-------|\n| type | text |\n| text | Found 41 files\n/workspace/autocxx/src/lib.rs\n/workspace/autocxx/macro/src/lib.rs\n/workspace/autocxx/parser/src/config.rs\n/workspace/autocxx/integration-tests/tests/integration_test.rs\n/workspace/autocxx/engine/src/lib.rs\n/workspace/autocxx/engine/src/parse_callbacks.rs\n/workspace/autocxx/engine/src/types.rs\n/workspace/autocxx/engine/src/conversion/parse/bindgen_sem