d with the current tasks if applicable |", "name": "TodoWrite", "tool_call_id": "TodoWrite:0"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" ?>\n<result>\n  <type>text</type>\n  <text>warning: hiding a lifetime that's elided elsewhere is confusing\n   --&gt; parser/src/config.rs:466:32\n    |\n466 |     pub fn get_makestring_name(&amp;self) -&gt; Cow&lt;str&gt; {\n    |                                ^^^^^     -------- the same lifetime is hidden here\n    |                                |\n    |                                the lifetime is elided here\n    |\n    = help: the same lifetime is referred to in inconsistent ways, making the signature confusing\n    = note: `#[warn(mismatched_lifetime_syntaxes)]` on by default\nhelp: use `'_` for type paths\n    |\n466 |     pub fn get_makestring_name(&amp;self) -&gt; Cow&lt;'_, str&gt; {\n    |                                              +++\n\nwarning: `autocxx-parser` (lib) generated 1 warning\n   Compiling autocxx-engine v0.17.4 (/workspace/autocxx/engine)\n   Compiling autocxx-macro v0.17.4 (/workspace/autocxx/macro)\nwarning: `autocxx-parser` (lib) generated 1 warning (1 duplicate)\nerror[E0599]: no method named `use_distinct_char16_t` found for struct `autocxx_bindgen::Builder` in the current scope\n   --&gt; engine/src/lib.rs:291:14\n    |\n278 |           let mut builder = bindgen::builder()\n    |  ___________________________-\n279 | |             .clang_args(make_clang_args(inc_dirs, extra_clang_args))\n280 | |             .derive_copy(false)\n281 | |             .derive_debug(false)\n...   |\n290 | |             .represent_cxx_operators(true)\n291 | |             .use_distinct_char16_t(true)\n    | |             -^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ method not found in `autocxx_bindgen::Builder`\n    | |_____________|\n    |\n\nFor more information about this error, try `rustc --explain E0599`.\nerror: could not compile `autocxx-engine` (lib) due to 1 previous error\nwarning: build failed, waiting for other jobs to finish...\nerror: could not compile `autocxx-engine` (lib) due to 1 previous error</text>\n</result>", "name": "Bash", "tool_call_id": "Bash:1"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "grep:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"-n\": true, \"OUTPUTMODE\": \"content\", \"path\": \"/workspace/autocxx/engine/Cargo.toml\", \"pattern\": \"autocxx-bindgen\"}"}}, {"id": "grep:1", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"-n\": true, \"OUTPUTMODE\": \"content\", \"path\": \"/workspace/autocxx/Cargo.lock\", \"pattern\": \"^name = \\\\\\\"autocxx-bindgen\\\\\\\"\", \"-A\": 2}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Ah, the issue is that the current version of autocxx-bindgen doesn't have the `use_distinct_char16_t` method. Looking back at the commit 707367a1, I see it also updated the autocxx-bindgen dependency to a newer version. Let me check what version is currently being used:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"Output_Text\": \"25:runtime = [ \\\"autocxx-bindgen/runtime\\\" ]\\n26:static = [ \\\"autocxx-bindgen/static\\\" ]\\n33:autocxx-bindgen = \\\"=0.59.14\\\"\\n34:#autocxx-bindgen = { git = \\\"https://github.com/adetaylor/rust-bindgen\\\", branch = \\\"denote-deleted-move-constructors\\\" }\"}", "name": "grep", "tool_call_id": "grep:0"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "84:name = \"autocxx-bindgen\"\n85-version = \"0.59.14\"\n86-source = \"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index\"", "name": "grep", "tool_call_id": "grep:1"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Bash:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"grep -r \\\"distinct.*char16\\\\|char16.*distinct\\\" /workspace/autocxx --include=\\\"*.rs\\\" 2>/dev/null | head -10\", \"description\": \"Search for char16 distinct references\"}"}}, {"id": "Bash:1", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cargo search autocxx-bindgen --limit 10 2>&1 | grep -A 2 \\\"autocxx-bindgen\\\"\", \"description\": \"Check available bindgen versions\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "So the current version is 0.59.14, but the fix requires 0.59.15 which has the `use_distinct_char16_t` method. However, I should check if there's a different approach that doesn't require upgrading the bindgen version.\n\nLooking at the commit again, it says:\n\"This involves absorbing a newer version of autocxx-bindgen which distinguishes char16_t from other types of u16.\"\n\nSo the newer bindgen version is what provides the distinction. Without it, bindgen will just convert char16_t to u16 like it does for uint16_t.\n\nLet me think about an alternative approach. The key issue is that when autocxx sees a Rust `u16` type and needs to convert it back to C++, it doesn't know if the original C++ type was `uint16_t` or `char16_t`.\n\nWait, let me look at the error message again more carefully:\n```\nurl_StringPieceReplacements_uint16_t_AutocxxConcrete\n```\n\nThis name is being generated from the Rust type. The problem is in `type_to_cpp` function which converts Rust types back to C++ types for naming concrete templates.\n\nActually, looking at the commit more carefully, the solution is indeed to use the newer bindgen version that marks char16_t types distinctly. Without that, there's no way to distinguish them from regular uint16_t at the Rust level.\n\nHowever, since I'm told the tests have been taken care of and just need to make the necessary changes, maybe the bindgen upgrade was already done separately? Let me check if maybe the method exists but under a different name, or if there's conditional compilation:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"CONTENT\": \"outputText: /workspace/autocxx/engine/src/lib.rs:            .use_distinct_char16_t(true)\"}", "name": "Bash", "tool_call_id": "Bash:0"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" ?>\n<result>\n  <RESULT_TEXT>autocxx-bindgen = &quot;0.73.0&quot;    # Automatically generates Rust FFI bindings to C and C++ libraries. Version adjusted for autocxx.\nnote: to learn more about a package, run `cargo info &lt;name&gt;`</RESULT_TEXT>\n</result>", "name": "Bash", "tool_call_id": "Bash:1"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Read:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/autocxx/engine/Cargo.toml\", \"LIMIT\": 10, \"offset\": 28}"}}, {"id": "Edit:1", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Edit", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/autocxx/engine/Cargo.toml\", \"oldstring\": \"proc-macro2 = \\\"1.0.11\\\"\\nquote = \\\"1.0\\\"\\nindoc = \\\"1.0\\\"\\nautocxx-bindgen = \\\"=0.59.14\\\"\\n#autocxx-bindgen = { git = \\\"https://github.com/adetaylor/rust-bindgen\\\", branch = \\\"denote-deleted-move-constructors\\\" }\", \"newstring\": \"proc-macro2 = \\\"1.0.11\\\"\\nquote = \\\"1.0\\\"\\nindoc = \\\"1.0\\\"\\nautocxx-bindgen = \\\"=0.59.15\\\"\\n#autocxx-bindgen = { git = \\\"https://github.com/adetaylor/rust-bindgen\\\", branch = \\\"denote-deleted-move-constructors\\\" }\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Interesting! The latest autocxx-bindgen is 0.73.0, but we're on 0.59.14. Since the fix requires 0.59.15 minimum (which has the `use_distinct_char16_t` method), I need to update the version. However, I should be conservative and just update to 0.59.15 as in the original commit, rather than jumping to a much newer version which might have breaking changes.\n\nLet me update the Cargo.toml:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "    28→[dependencies]\n    29→log = \"0.4\"\n    30→proc-macro2 = \"1.0.11\"\n    31→quote = \"1.0\"\n    32→indoc = \"1.0\"\n    33→autocxx-bindgen = \"=0.59.14\"\n    34→#autocxx-bindgen = { git = \"https://github.com/adetaylor/rust-bindgen\", branch = \"denote-deleted-move-constructors\" }\n    35→itertools = \"0.10.3\"\n    36→cc = { version = \"1.0\", optional = true }\n    37→# Note: Keep the patch-level version of cxx-gen and cxx in sync.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:0"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "The file /workspace/autocxx/engine/Cargo.toml has been updated. Here's the result of running `cat -n` on a snippet of the edited file:\n    26→static = [ \"autocxx-bindgen/static\" ]\n    27→\n    28→[dependencies]\n    29→log = \"0.4\"\n    30→proc-macro2 = \"1.0.11\"\n    31→quote = \"1.0\"\n    32→indoc = \"1.0\"\n    33→autocxx-bindgen = \"=0.59.15\"\n    34→#autocxx-bindgen = { git = \"https://github.com/adetaylor/rust-bindgen\", branch = \"denote-deleted-move-constructors\" }\n    35→itertools = \"0.10.3\"\n    36→cc = { version = \"1.0\", optional = true }\n    37→# Note: Keep the patch-level version of cxx-gen and cxx in sync.\n    38→# There can be interdependencies between the code generated by cxx-gen and\n\n<system-reminder>\nThe TodoWrite tool hasn't been used recently. If you're working on tasks that would benefit from tracking progress, consider using the TodoWrite tool to track progress. Also consider cleaning up the todo list if has become stale and no longer matches what you are working on. Only use it if it's relevant to the current work. This is just a gentle reminder - ignore if not applicable.\n\n</system-reminder>", "name": "Edit", "tool_call_id": "Edit:1"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Bash:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cargo build --workspace --exclude autocxx-mdbook-preprocessor 2>&1 | tail -80\", \"description\": \"Build with updated bindgen version\", \"TIMEOUT\": 120000}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Good! Now let me try building again:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "14 |     Run(std::io::Error),\n   |     --- ^^^^^^^^^