\\".  For plain \\\"T*\\\", we could just let the\\n 10516→\\t machinery in cp_build_function_call convert it -- but if the\\n 10517→\\t type is \\\"cv-qualified T *\\\", then we need to convert it\\n 10518→\\t before passing it in, to avoid spurious errors.  */\\n 10519→      addr = build_nop (ptr_type_node, addr);\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:1"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "4500→\t}\n  4501→\n  4502→      guard_if_stmt = begin_if_stmt ();\n  4503→      finish_if_stmt_cond (guard_cond, guard_if_stmt);\n  4504→\n  4505→      if (flag_use_cxa_atexit)\n  4506→\t/* Set the GUARD now.  */\n  4507→\tfinish_expr_stmt (set_guard (guard));\n  4508→    }\n  4509→\n  4510→  /* Perform the initialization or destruction.  */\n  4511→  if (initp)\n  4512→    {\n  4513→      if (init)\n  4514→\t{\n  4515→\t  finish_expr_stmt (init);\n  4516→\t  if (sanitize_flags_p (SANITIZE_ADDRESS, decl))\n  4517→\t    if (varpool_node *vnode = varpool_node::get (decl))\n  4518→\t      vnode->dynamically_initialized = 1;\n  4519→\t}\n  4520→\n  4521→      /* If we're using __cxa_atexit, register a function that calls the\n  4522→\t destructor for the object.  */\n  4523→      if (flag_use_cxa_atexit)\n  4524→\tfinish_expr_stmt (register_dtor_fn (decl));\n  4525→    }\n  4526→  else\n  4527→    finish_expr_stmt (build_cleanup (decl));\n  4528→\n  4529→  /* Finish the guard if-stmt, if necessary.  */\n  4530→  if (guard_if_stmt)\n  4531→    {\n  4532→      finish_then_clause (guard_if_stmt);\n  4533→      finish_if_stmt (guard_if_stmt);\n  4534→    }\n  4535→\n  4536→  /* Now that we're done with DECL we don't need to pretend to be a\n  4537→     member of its class any longer.  */\n  4538→  DECL_CONTEXT (current_function_decl) = NULL_TREE;\n  4539→  DECL_STATIC_FUNCTION_P (current_function_decl) = 0;\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n\n<system-reminder>\nThe TodoWrite tool hasn't been used recently. If you're working on tasks that would benefit from tracking progress, consider using the TodoWrite tool to track progress. Also consider cleaning up the todo list if has become stale and no longer matches what you are working on. Only use it if it's relevant to the current work. This is just a gentle reminder - ignore if not applicable.\n\n</system-reminder>", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:2"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "grep:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"pattern\": \"^register_dtor_fn\", \"path\": \"/home/gcc/gcc/cp\", \"Outputmode\": \"content\", \"-n\": true}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Now I can see the issue. At line 4524 in decl2.cc, `register_dtor_fn` is being called and its result is being used with `finish_expr_stmt`. The `register_dtor_fn` function is trying to add a statement (via `add_stmt`), but the problem is that the statement list stack is empty.\n\nLet me look at the `register_dtor_fn` function more closely to understand what it does:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "result=/home/gcc/gcc/cp/decl.cc:10433:register_dtor_fn (tree decl)", "name": "grep", "tool_call_id": "grep:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Read:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/home/gcc/gcc/cp/decl.cc\", \"Limit\": 150, \"OFFSET\": 10433}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Let me read the full `register_dtor_fn` function."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"TEXTRESULT\": \"type: te