nding\", \"activeForm\": \"Updating handle_tls_init\"}]}"}}], "reasoning_content": "So current HEAD is from 2025-02-25 09:26:46, and the fix commit 86a4af27933 is from 2025-02-25 09:29:39, which is just 3 minutes later! So the current HEAD doesn't have the fix yet.\n\nNow I understand the situation. The issue is that we need to apply the fix similar to what was done in commit 86a4af27933. Let me check the current state of the code more carefully to understand what needs to be fixed. Let me create a todo list for this:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"Output\": \"type=text\\ntext=The todos have been successfully modified. Please proceed with the current tasks if applicable and ensure you use the todo list to track your progress.\"}", "name": "TodoWrite", "tool_call_id": "TodoWrite:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Now let me look at the current state of the code to understand what changes are needed:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Read:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/home/gcc/gcc/cp/cp-tree.h\", \"Limit\": 10, \"OFFSET\": 7200}"}}, {"id": "grep:1", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"-n\": true, \"Outputmode\": \"content\", \"path\": \"/home/gcc/gcc/cp/decl.cc\", \"pattern\": \"^static tree start_cleanup_fn\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Let me read the relevant sections more carefully to understand the issue:\n\nThe problem is that when `register_dtor_fn` is called from `one_static_initialization_or_destruction` in the context of OpenMP target destructor generation, it creates a cleanup function and tries to add statements to it. However, if this is being done in a context where the stmt_list_stack is empty (which shouldn't normally happen but can occur in certain OpenMP declare target scenarios), it causes an ICE.\n\nThe fix is to:\n1. Add an `omp_target` parameter to `register_dtor_fn` and related functions\n2. When `omp_target` is true, use `__omp_tcf` prefix instead of `__tcf` and add OpenMP attributes\n3. This differentiates between host and target cleanup functions\n\nLet me start making the changes:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" ?>\n<result>\n  <content>  7200→extern int wrapup_namespace_globals\t\t();\n  7201→extern tree create_implicit_typedef\t\t(tree, tree);\n  7202→extern int local_variable_p\t\t\t(const_tree);\n  7203→extern tree get_cxa_atexit_fn_ptr_type\t\t();\n  7204→extern tree register_dtor_fn\t\t\t(tree);\n  7205→extern tmpl_spec_kind current_tmpl_spec_kind\t(int);\n  7206→extern tree cxx_builtin_function\t\t(tree decl);\n  7207→extern tree cxx_builtin_function_ext_scope\t(tree decl);\n  7208→extern tree cxx_simulate_builtin_function_decl\t(tree);\n  7209→extern tree check_elaborated_type_specifier\t(enum tag_types, tree, bool);\n\n&lt;system-reminder&gt;\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\n&lt;/system-reminder&gt;\n</content>\n</result>", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:0"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "type=text\ntext=99:static tree start_cleanup_fn (tree, bool);", "name": "grep", "tool_call_id": "grep:1"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Read:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/home/gcc/gcc/cp/decl.cc\", \"Limit\": 100, \"OFFSET\": 10000}"}}], "reasoning_content": "I can see the declaration. Let me read the actual implementation of `start_cleanup_fn`:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "| Key | Value |\n|-----|-------|\n| type | text |\n| text |  10000→\t}\n 10001→      eltscnt = tree_to_uhwi (tsize);\n 10002→      if (count != eltscnt)\n 10003→\tgoto cnt_mismatch;\n 10004→      if (test_p)\n 10005→\treturn true;\n 10006→      if (!processing_template_decl && DECL_DECOMP_BASE (decl))\n 10007→\t{\n 10008→\t  /* For structured bindings used in conditions we need to evaluate\n 10009→\t     the conversion of decl (aka e in the standard) to bool or\n 10010→\t     integral/enumeral type (the latter for switch conditions)\n 10011→\t     before the get methods.  */\n 10012→\t  tree cond = convert_from_reference (decl);\n 10013→\t  if (integer_onep (DECL_DECOMP_BASE (decl)))\n 10014→\t    /* switch condition.  */\n 10015→\t    cond = build_expr_type_conversion (WANT_INT \\| WANT_ENUM,\n 10016→\t\t\t\t\t       cond, true);\n 10017→\t  else\n 10018→\t    /* if/while/for condition.  */\n 10019→\t    cond = contextual_conv_bool (cond, tf_warning_or_error);\n 10020→\t  if (cond && !error_operand_p (cond))\n 10021→\t    {\n 10022→\t      /* Wrap that value into a TARGET_EXPR, emit it right\n 10023→\t\t away and save for later uses in the cp_parse_condition\n 10024→\t\t or its instantiation.  */\n 10025→\t      cond = get_internal_target_expr (cond);\n 10026→\t      add_stmt (cond);\n 10027→\t      DECL_DECOMP_BASE (decl) = cond;\n 10028→\t    }\n 10029→\t}\n 10030→      int save_read = DECL_READ_P (decl);\n 10031→      for (unsigned i = 0; i < count; ++i)\n 10032→\t{\n 10033→\t  location_t sloc = input_location;\n 10034→\t  location_t dloc = DECL_SOURCE_LOCATION (v[i]);\n 10035→\n 10036→\t  input_location = dloc;\n 10037→\t  tree init = get_tuple_decomp_init (decl, i);\n 10038→\t  tree eltype = (init == error_mark_node ? error_mark_node\n 10039→\t\t\t : get_tuple_element_type (type, i));\n 10040→\t  input_location = sloc;\n 10041→\n 10042→\t  if (VOID_TYPE_P (eltype))\n 10043→\t    {\n 10044→\t      error (\"%<std::tuple_element<%u, %T>::type%> is %<void%>\",\n 10045→\t\t     i, type);\n 10046→\t      eltype = error_mark_node;\n 10047→\t    }\n 10048→\t  if (init == error_mark_node \\|\\| eltype == error_mark_node)\n 10049→\t    {\n 10050→\t      inform (dloc, \"in initialization of structured binding \"\n 10051→\t\t      \"variable %qD\", v[i]);\n 10052→\t      goto error_out;\n 10053→\t   