Expected: First cell empty (just has \\\\def), second cell shows \\\"1\\\" (original definition)\\nSuccessfully rendered!\\nRendered values in cells: [ '2', '2' ]\\n✗ FAIL: Expected only \\\"1\\\" in second cell, got: [ '2', '2' ]\\n\\n=== Script completed successfully ===\"}", "name": "Bash", "tool_call_id": "Bash:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "TodoWrite:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "TodoWrite", "arguments": "{\"todos\": [{\"activeForm\": \"Exploring the repository\", \"content\": \"Explore the repository to understand its structure\", \"status\": \"completed\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Creating reproduce_issue.js script\", \"content\": \"Create reproduce_issue.js script to confirm the bug\", \"status\": \"completed\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Understanding parseArray implementation\", \"content\": \"Understand how parseArray works and where scoping happens\", \"status\": \"in_progress\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Fixing scoping issue\", \"content\": \"Fix the scoping issue in array environments\", \"status\": \"pending\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Testing the fix\", \"content\": \"Test the fix with reproduce script\", \"status\": \"pending\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Cleaning up temporary files\", \"content\": \"Clean up temporary files\", \"status\": \"pending\"}]}"}}, {"id": "Read:1", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/KaTeX/src/Parser.js\", \"limit\": 50}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Perfect! The reproduce script confirms the issue:\n\n1. Test case `\\def\\x{1}\\begin{matrix}\\def\\x{2}\\x&\\x\\end{matrix}\\x` renders as `222222` instead of the expected `211`\n2. Test case `\\def\\x{1}\\begin{matrix}\\def\\x{2}&\\x\\end{matrix}` renders `['2', '2']` instead of `['2', '1']` (or actually empty first cell and '1' in second)\n\nThis confirms that:\n- The `\\def\\x{2}` inside the first cell is leaking to the second cell and outside the matrix\n- Array cells are NOT creating proper scoping groups for macro definitions\n\nNow let me update the todo and start investigating the code to understand how scoping works:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"textresult\": \"Now that your todos are successfully modified, please proceed with any applicable current tasks and continue to use the todo list to track your progress.\"}", "name": "TodoWrite", "tool_call_id": "TodoWrite:0"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "     1→// @flow\n     2→/* eslint no-constant-condition:0 */\n     3→import functions from \"./functions\";\n     4→import MacroExpander, {implicitCommands} from \"./MacroExpander\";\n     5→import symbols, {ATOMS, extraLatin} from \"./symbols\";\n     6→import {validUnit} from \"./units\";\n     7→import {supportedCodepoint} from \"./unicodeScripts\";\n     8→import unicodeAccents from \"./unicodeAccents\";\n     9→import unicodeSymbols from \"./unicodeSymbols\";\n    10→import {checkNodeType} from \"./parseNode\";\n    11→import ParseError from \"./ParseError\";\n    12→import {combiningDiacriticalMarksEndRegex} from \"./Lexer\";\n    13→import Settings from \"./Settings\";\n    14→import SourceLocation from \"./SourceLocation\";\n    15→import {Token} from \"./Token\";\n    16→import type {ParseNode, AnyParseNode, SymbolParseNode, UnsupportedCmdParseNode}\n    17→    from \"./parseNode\";\n    18→import type {Atom, Group} from \"./symbols\";\n    19→import type {Mode, ArgType, BreakToken} from \"./types\";\n    20→import type {FunctionContext, FunctionSpec} from \"./defineFunction\";\n    21→import type {EnvSpec} from \"./defineEnvironment\";\n    22→\n    23→/**\n    24→ * This file contains the parser used to parse out a TeX expression from the\n    25→ * input. Since TeX isn't context-free, standard parsers don't work particularly\n    26→ * well.\n    27→ *\n    28→ * The strategy of this parser is as such:\n    29→ *\n    30→ * The main functions (the `.parse...` ones) take a position in the current\n    31→ * parse string to parse tokens from. The lexer (found in Lexer.js, stored at\n    32→ * this.gullet.lexer) also supports pulling out tokens at arbitrary places. When\n    33→ * individual tokens are needed at a position, the lexer is called to pull out a\n    34→ * token, which is then used.\n    35→ *\n    36→ * The parser has a property called \"mode\" indicating the mode that\n    37→ * the parser is currently in. Currently it has to be one of \"math\" or\n    38→ * \"text\", which denotes whether the current environment is a math-y\n    39→ * one or a text-y one (e.g. inside \\text). Currently, this serves to\n    40→ * limit the functions which can be used in text mode.\n    41→ *\n    42→ * The main functions then return an object which contains the useful data that\n    43→ * was parsed at its given point, and a new position at the end of the parsed\n    44→ * data. The main functions can call each other and continue the parsing by\n    45→ * using the returned position as a new starting point.\n    46→ *\n    47→ * There are also extra `.handle...` functions, which pull out some reused\n    48→ * functionality into self-contained functions.\n    49→ *\n    50→ * The functions return ParseNodes.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:1"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Read:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/KaTeX/src/MacroExpander.js\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Let me look at how the MacroExpander and gullet work, particularly the `beginGroup` and `endGroup` functions:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "     1→// @flow\n     2→/**\n     3→ * This file contains the “gullet” where macros are expanded\n     4→ * until only non-macro tokens remain.\n     5→ */\n     6→\n     7→import functions from \"./functions\";\n     8→import symbols from \"./symbols\";\n     9→import Lexer from \"./Lexer\";\n    10→import {Token} from \"./Token\";\n    11→import type {Mode} from \"./types\";\n    12→import ParseError from \"./ParseError\";\n    13→import Namespace from \"./Namespace\";\n    14→import builtinMacros from \"./macros\";\n    15→\n    16→import type {MacroContextInterface, MacroDefinition, MacroExpansion}\n    17→    from \"./macros\";\n    18→import type Settings from \"./Settings\";\n    19→\n    20→// List of commands that act like macros but aren't defined as a macro,\n    21→// function, or symbol.  Used in `isDefined`.\n    22→export const implicitCommands = {\n    23→    \"\\\\relax\": true,     // MacroExpander.js\n    24→    \"^\": true,           // Parser.js\n    25→    \"_\": true,           // Parser.js\n    26→    \"\\\\limits\": true,    // Parser.js\n    27→    \"\\\\nolimits\": true,  // Parser.js\n    28→};\n    29→\n    30→export default class MacroExpander implements MacroContextInterface {\n    31→    settings: Settings;\n    32→    expansionCount: number;\n    33→    lexer: Lexer;\n    34→    macros: Namespace<MacroDefinition>;\n    35→    stack: Token[];\n    36→    mode: Mode;\n    37→\n    38→    constructor(input: string, settings: Settings, mode: Mode) {\n    39→        this.settings = settings;\n    40→        this.expansionCount = 0;\n    41→        this.feed(input);\n    42→        // Make new global namespace\n    43→        this.macros = new Namespace(builtinMacros, settings.macros);\n    44→        this.mode = mode;\n    45→        this.stack = []; // contains tokens in REVERSE order\n    46→    }\n    47→\n    48→    /**\n    49→     * Feed a new input string to the same MacroExpander\n    50→     * (with existing macros etc.).\n    51→     */\n    52→    feed(input: string) {\n    53→        this.lexer = new Lexer(input, this.settings);\n    54→    }\n    55→\n    56→    /**\n    57→     * Switches between \"text\" and \"math\" modes.\n    58→     */\n    59→    switchMode(newMode: Mode) {\n    60→        this.mode = newMode;\n    61→    }\n    62→\n    63→    /**\n    64→     * Start a new group nesting within all namespaces.\n    65→     */\n    66→    beginGroup() {\n    67→        this.macros.beginGroup();\n    68→    }\n    69→\n    70→    /**\n    71→     * End current group nesting within all namespaces.\n    72→     */\n    73→    endGroup() {\n    74→        this.macros.endGroup();\n    75→    }\n    76→\n    77→    /**\n    78→     * Returns the topmost token on the stack, without expanding it.\n    79→     * Similar in behavior to TeX's `\\futurelet`.\n    80→     */\n    81→    future(): Token {\n    82→        if (this.stack.length === 0) {\n    83→            this.pushToken(this.lexer.lex());\n    84→        }\n    85→        return this.stack[this.stack.length - 1];\n    86→    }\n    87→\n    88→    /**\n    89→     * Remove and return the next unexpanded token.\n    90→     */\n    91→    popToken(): Token {\n    92→        this.future();  // ensure non-empty stack\n    93→        return this.stack.pop();\n    94→    }\n    95→\n    96→    /**\n    97→     * Add a given token to the token stack.  In particular, this get be used\n    98→     * to put back a token returned from one of the other methods.\n    99→     */\n   100→    pushToken(token: Token) {\n   101→        this.stack.push(token);\n   102→    }\n   103→\n   104→    /**\n   105→     * Append an array of tokens to the token stack.\n   106→     */\n   107→    pushTokens(tokens: Token[]) {\n   108→        this.stack.push(...tokens);\n   109→    }\n   110→\n   111→    /**\n   112→     * Consume all following space tokens, without expansion.\n   113→     */\n   114→    consumeSpaces() {\n   115→        for (;;) {\n   116→            const token = this.future();\n   117→            if (token.text === \" \") {\n   118→                this.stack.pop();\n   119→            } else {\n   120→                break;\n   121→            }\n   122→        }\n   123→    }\n   124→\n   125→    /**\n   126→     * Consume the specified number of arguments from the token stream,\n   127→     * and return the resulting array of arguments.\n   128→     */\n   129→    consumeArgs(numArgs: number): Token[][] {\n   130→        const args: Token[][] = [];\n   131→        // obtain arguments, either single token or balanced {…} group\n   132→        for (let i = 0; i < numArgs; ++i) {\n   133→            this.consumeSpaces();  // ignore spaces before each argument\n   134→            const startOfArg = this.popToken();\n   135→            if (startOfArg.text === \"{\") {\n   136→                const arg: Token[] = [];\n   137→                let depth = 1;\n   138→                while (depth !== 0) {\n   139→                    const tok = this.popToken();\n   140→                    arg.push(tok);\n   141→                    if (tok.text === \"{\") {\n   142→                        ++depth;\n   143→                    } else if (tok.text === \"}\") {\n   144→                        --depth;\n   145→                    } else if (tok.text === \"EOF\") {\n   146→                        throw new ParseError(\n   147→                            \"End of input in macro argument\",\n   148→                            startOfArg);\n   149→                    }\n   150→                }\n   151→                arg.pop(); // remove last }\n   152→                arg.reverse(); // like above, to fit in with stack order\n   153→                args[i] = arg;\n   154→            } else if (startOfArg.text === \"EOF\") {\n   155→                throw new ParseError(\n   156→                    \"End of input expecting macro argument\");\n   157→            } else {\n   158→                args[i] = [startOfArg];\n   159→            }\n   160→        }\n   161→        return args;\n   162→    }\n   163→\n   164→    /**\n   165→     * Expand the next token only once if possible.\n   166→     *\n   167→     * If the token is expanded, the resulting tokens will be pushed onto\n   168→     * the stack in reverse order and will be returned as an array,\n   169→     * also in reverse order.\n   170→     *\n   171→     * If not, the next token will be returned without removing it\n   172→     * from the stack.  This case can be detected by a `Token` return value\n   173→     * instead of an `Array` return value.\n   174→     *\n   175→     * In either case, the next token will be on the top of the stack,\n   176→     * or the stack will be empty.\n   177→     *\n   178→     * Used to implement `expandAfterFuture` and `expandNextToken`.\n   179→     *\n   180→     * At the moment, macro expansion doesn't handle delimited macros,\n   181→     * i.e. things like those defined by \\def\\foo#1\\end{…}.\n   182→     * See the TeX book page 202ff. for details on how those should behave.\n   183→     */\n   184→    expandOnce(): Token | Token[] {\n   185→        const topToken = this.popToken();\n   186→        const name = topToken.text;\n   187→        const expansion = this._getExpansion(name);\n   188→        if (expansion == null) { // mainly checking for undefined here\n   189→            // Fully expanded\n   190→            this.pushToken(topToken);\n   191→            return topToken;\n   192→        }\n   193→        this.expansionCount++;\n   194→        if (this.expansionCount > this.settings.maxExpand) {\n   195→            throw new ParseError(\"Too many expansions: infinite loop or \" +\n   196→                \"need to increase maxExpand setting\");\n   197→        }\n   198→        let tokens = expansion.tokens;\n   199→        if (expansion.numArgs) {\n   200→            const args = this.consumeArgs(expansion.numArgs);\n   201→            // paste arguments in place of the placeholders\n   202→            tokens = tokens.slice(); // make a shallow copy\n   203→            for (let i = tokens.length - 1; i >= 0; --i) {\n   204→                let tok = tokens[i];\n   205→                if (tok.text === \"#\") {\n   206→                    if (i === 0) {\n   207→                        throw new ParseError(\n   208→                            \"Incomplete placeholder at end of macro body\",\n   209→                            tok);\n   210→                    }\n   211→                    tok = tokens[--i]; // next token on stack\n   212→                    if (tok.text === \"#\") { // ## → #\n   213→                        tokens.splice(i + 1, 1); // drop first #\n   214→                    } else if (/^[1-9]$/.test(tok.text)) {\n   215→                        // replace the placeholder with the indicated argument\n   216→                        tokens.splice(i, 2, ...args[+tok.text - 1]);\n   217→                    } else {\n   218→                        throw new ParseError(\n   219→                            \"Not a valid argument number\",\n   220→                            tok);\n   221→                    }\n   222→                }\n   223→            }\n   224→        }\n   225→        // Concatenate expansion onto top of stack.\n   226→        this.pushTokens(tokens);\n   227→        return tokens;\n   228→    }\n   229→\n   230→    /**\n   231→     * Expand the next token only once (if possible), and return the resulting\n   232→     * top token on the stack (without removing anything from the stack).\n   233→     * Similar in behavior to TeX's `\\expandafter\\futurelet`.\n   234→     * Equivalent to expandOnce() followed by future().\n   235→     */\n   236→    expandAfterFuture(): Token {\n   237→        this.expandOnce();\n   238→        return this.future();\n   239→    }\n   240→\n   241→    /**\n   242→     * Recursively expand first token, then return first non-expandable token.\n   243→     */\n   244→    expandNextToken(): Token {\n   245→        for (;;) {\n   246→            const expanded = this.expandOnce();\n   247→            // expandOnce returns Token if and only if it's fully expanded.\n   248→            if (expanded instanceof Token) {\n   249→                // \\relax stops the expansion, but shouldn't get returned (a\n   250→                // null return value couldn't get implemented as a function).\n   251→                if (expanded.text === \"\\\\relax\") {\n   252→                    this.stack.pop();\n   253→                } else {\n   254→                    return this.stack.pop();  // === expanded\n   255→                }\n   256→            }\n   257→        }\n   258→\n   259→        // Flow unable to figure out that this pathway is impossible.\n   260→        // https://github.com/facebook/flow/issues/4808\n   261→        throw new Error(); // eslint-disable-line no-unreachable\n   262→    }\n   263→\n   264→    /**\n   265→     * Fully expand the given macro name and return the resulting list of\n   266→     * tokens, or return `undefined` if no such macro is defined.\n   267→     */\n   268→    expandMacro(name: string): Token[] | void {\n   269→        if (!this.macros.get(name)) {\n   270→            return undefined;\n   271→        }\n   272→        const output = [];\n   273→        const oldStackLength = this.stack.length;\n   274→        this.pushToken(new Token(name));\n   275→        while (this.stack.length > oldStackLength) {\n   276→            const expanded = this.expandOnce();\n   277→            // expandOnce returns Token if and only if it's fully expanded.\n   278→            if (expanded instanceof Token) {\n   279→                output.push(this.stack.pop());\n   280→            }\n   281→        }\n   282→        return output;\n   283→    }\n   284→\n   285→    /**\n   286→     * Fully expand the given macro name and return the result as a string,\n   287→     * or return `undefined` if no such macro is defined.\n   288→     */\n   289→    expandMacroAsText(name: string): string | void {\n   290→        const tokens = this.expandMacro(name);\n   291→        if (tokens) {\n   292→            return tokens.map((token) => token.text).join(\"\");\n   293→        } else {\n   294→            return tokens;\n   295→        }\n   296→    }\n   297→\n   298→    /**\n   299→     * Returns the expanded macro as a reversed array of tokens and a macro\n   300→     * argument count.  Or returns `null` if no such macro.\n   301→     */\n   302→    _getExpansion(name: string): ?MacroExpansion {\n   303→        const definition = this.macros.get(name);\n   304→        if (definition == null) { // mainly checking for undefined here\n   305→            return definition;\n   306→        }\n   307→        const expansion =\n   308→            typeof definition === \"function\" ? definition(this) : definition;\n   309→        if (typeof expansion === \"string\") {\n   310→            let numArgs = 0;\n   311→            if (expansion.indexOf(\"#\") !== -1) {\n   312→                const stripped = expansion.replace(/##/g, \"\");\n   313→                while (stripped.indexOf(\"#\" + (numArgs + 1)) !== -1) {\n   314→                    ++numArgs;\n   315→                }\n   316→            }\n   317→            const bodyLexer = new Lexer(expansion, this.settings);\n   318→            const tokens = [];\n   319→            let tok = bodyLexer.lex();\n   320→            while (tok.text !== \"EOF\") {\n   321→                tokens.push(tok);\n   322→                tok = bodyLexer.lex();\n   323→            }\n   324→            tokens.reverse(); // to fit in with stack using push and pop\n   325→            const expanded = {tokens, numArgs};\n   326→            return expanded;\n   327→        }\n   328→\n   329→        return expansion;\n   330→    }\n   331→\n   332→    /**\n   333→     * Determine whether a command is currently \"defined\" (has some\n   334→     * functionality), meaning that it's a macro (in the current group),\n   335→     * a function, a symbol, or one of the special commands listed in\n   336→     * `implicitCommands`.\n   337→     */\n   338→    isDefined(name: string): boolean {\n   339→        return this.macros.has(name) ||\n   340→            functions.hasOwnProperty(name) ||\n   341→            symbols.math.hasOwnProperty(name) ||\n   342→            symbols.text.hasOwnProperty(name) ||\n   343→            implicitCommands.hasOwnProperty(name);\n   344→    }\n   345→}\n   346→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Read:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/KaTeX/src/Namespace.js\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Good! I can see that MacroExpander has `beginGroup()` and `endGroup()` methods that delegate to `this.macros` which is a Namespace object. Let me look at the Namespace class:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"text_result\": \"     1→// @flow\\n     2→\\n     3→/**\\n     4→ * A `Namespace` refers to a space of nameable things like macros or lengths,\\n     5→ * which can be `set` either globally or local to a nested group, using an\\n     6→ * undo stack similar to how TeX implements this functionality.\\n     7→ * Performance-wise, `get` and local `set` take constant time, while global\\n     8→ * `set` takes time proportional to the depth of group nesting.\\n     9→ */\\n    10→\\n    11→import ParseError from \\\"./ParseError\\\";\\n    12→\\n    13→export type Mapping<Value> = {[string]: Value};\\n    14→\\n    15→export default class Namespace<Value> {\\n    16→    current: Mapping<Value>;\\n    17→    builtins: Mapping<Value>;\\n    18→    undefStack: Mapping<Value>[];\\n    19→\\n    20→    /**\\n    21→     * Both arguments are optional.  The first argument is an object of\\n    22→     * built-in mappings which never change.  The second argument is an object\\n    23→     * of initial (global-level) mappings, which will constantly change\\n    24→     * according to any global/top-level `set`s done.\\n    25→     */\\n    26→    constructor(builtins: Mapping<Value> = {},\\n    27→                globalMacros: Mapping<Value> = {}) {\\n    28→        this.current = globalMacros;\\n    29→        this.builtins = builtins;\\n    30→        this.undefStack = [];\\n    31→    }\\n    32→\\n    33→    /**\\n    34→     * Start a new nested group, affecting future local `set`s.\\n    35→     */\\n    36→    beginGroup() {\\n    37→        this.undefStack.push({});\\n    38→    }\\n    39→\\n    40→    /**\\n    41→     * End current nested group, restoring values before the group began.\\n    42→     */\\n    43→    endGroup() {\\n    44→        if (this.undefStack.length === 0) {\\n    45→            throw new ParseError(\\\"Unbalanced namespace destruction: attempt \\\" +\\n    46→                \\\"to pop global namespace; please report this as a bug\\\");\\n    47→        }\\n    48→        const undefs = this.undefStack.pop();\\n    49→        for (const undef in undefs) {\\n    50→            if (undefs.hasOwnProperty(undef)) {\\n    51→                if (undefs[undef] === undefined) {\\n    52→                    delete this.current[undef];\\n    53→                } else {\\n    54→                    this.current[undef] = undefs[undef];\\n    55→                }\\n    56→            }\\n    57→        }\\n    58→    }\\n    59→\\n    60→    /**\\n    61→     * Detect whether `name` has a definition.  Equivalent to\\n    62→     * `get(name) != null`.\\n    63→     */\\n    64→    has(name: string): boolean {\\n    65→        return this.current.hasOwnProperty(name) ||\\n    66→               this.builtins.hasOwnProperty(name);\\n    67→    }\\n    68→\\n    69→    /**\\n    70→     * Get the current value of a name, or `undefined` if there is no value.\\n    71→     *\\n    72→     * Note: Do not use `if (namespace.get(...))` to detect whether a macro\\n    73→     * is defined, as the definition may be the empty string which evaluates\\n    74→     * to `false` in JavaScript.  Use `if (namespace.get(...) != null)` or\\n    75→     * `if (namespace.has(...))`.\\n    76→     */\\n    77→    get(name: string): ?Value {\\n    78→        if (this.current.hasOwnProperty(name)) {\\n    79→            return this.current[name];\\n    80→        } else {\\n    81→            return this.builtins[name];\\n    82→        }\\n    83→    }\\n    84→\\n    85→    /**\\n    86→     * Set the current value of a name, and optionally set it globally too.\\n    87→     * Local set() sets the current value and (when appropriate) adds an undo\\n    88→     * operation to the undo stack.  Global set() may change the undo\\n    89→     * operation at every level, so takes time linear in their number.\\n    90→     */\\n    91→    set(name: string, value: Value, global: boolean = false) {\\n    92→        if (global) {\\n    93→            // Global set is equivalent to setting in all groups.  Simulate this\\n    94→            // by destroying any undos currently scheduled for this name,\\n    95→            // and adding an undo with the *new* value (in case it later gets\\n    96→            // locally reset within this environment).\\n    97→            for (let i = 0; i < this.undefStack.length; i++) {\\n    98→                delete this.undefStack[i][name];\\n    99→            }\\n   100→            if (this.undefStack.length > 0) {\\n   101→                this.undefStack[this.undefStack.length - 1][name] = value;\\n   102→            }\\n   103→        } else {\\n   104→            // Undo this set at end of this group (possibly to `undefined`),\\n   105→            // unless an undo is already in place, in which case that older\\n   106→            // value is the correct one.\\n   107→            const top = this.undefStack[this.undefStack.length - 1];\\n   108→            if (top && !top.hasOwnProperty(name)) {\\n   109→                top[name] = this.current[name];\\n   110→            }\\n   111→        }\\n   112→        this.current[name] = value;\\n   113→    }\\n   114→}\\n   115→\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Grep:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Grep", "arguments": "{\"-n\": true, \"output-mode\": \"content\", \"pattern\": \"defineFunction.*begin\", \"path\": \"/workspace/KaTeX/src/functions\"}"}}, {"id": "Read:1", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/KaTeX/src/functions/environment.js\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Perfect! Now I understand how scoping works in KaTeX:\n\n1. The `Namespace` class maintains an `undefStack` which is a stack of undo operations\n2. `beginGroup()` pushes a new undo level onto the stack\n3. `endGroup()` pops the undo level and restores previous values\n4. When `set()` is called locally (global=false), it records the previous value in the current undo level\n\nNow let me look at the `parseArray` function again to see where groups are created. Looking back at the code in `/workspace/KaTeX/src/environments/array.js`:\n\n- Line 65: `parser.gullet.beginGroup();` - creates a group for the whole array\n- Line 83: `parser.gullet.beginGroup();` - creates a group for the first cell\n- Line 95: `let cell = parser.parseExpression(false, \"\\\\cr\");` - parses the cell content\n- Line 96: `parser.gullet.endGroup();` - ends the cell group\n- Line 97: `parser.gullet.beginGroup();` - starts a new cell group\n\nThe problem described in the TODO is that when parsing starts for the environment, the `\\begin{matrix}` command consumes the `{matrix}` argument, which means any `\\def` commands at the start of the first cell get executed BEFORE the cell group is created.\n\nLet me trace through what happens with `\\def\\x{1}\\begin{matrix}\\def\\x{2}&\\x\\end{matrix}`:\n\n1. Parse `\\def\\x{1}` - defines x as 1 globally (or at current level)\n2. Parse `\\begin{matrix}` - this triggers the environment handler\n3. The environment handler is called\n4. Line 65: beginGroup() for the array\n5. Line 66: macros.set(\"\\\\\\\\\", \"\\\\cr\")\n6. Line 83: beginGroup() for first cell\n\n\n7. I'm curious about how the `{matrix}` argument is processed and whether it triggers any macro expansions that could interfere with subsequent parsing.\n\nThe potential issue seems to revolve around the timing of argument consumption and group creation during environment initialization. Macro definitions before the environment might interact unexpectedly with the parsing process."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "     1→// @flow\n     2→import defineFunction from \"../defineFunction\";\n     3→import ParseError from \"../ParseError\";\n     4→import {assertNodeType} from \"../parseNode\";\n     5→import environments from \"../environments\";\n     6→\n     7→// Environment delimiters. HTML/MathML rendering is defined in the corresponding\n     8→// defineEnvironment definitions.\n     9→// $FlowFixMe, \"environment\" handler returns an environment ParseNode\n    10→defineFunction({\n    11→    type: \"environment\",\n    12→    names: [\"\\\\begin\", \"\\\\end\"],\n    13→    props: {\n    14→        numArgs: 1,\n    15→        argTypes: [\"text\"],\n    16→    },\n    17→    handler({parser, funcName}, args) {\n    18→        const nameGroup = args[0];\n    19→        if (nameGroup.type !== \"ordgroup\") {\n    20→            throw new ParseError(\"Invalid environment name\", nameGroup);\n    21→        }\n    22→        let envName = \"\";\n    23→        for (let i = 0; i < nameGroup.body.length; ++i) {\n    24→            envName += assertNodeType(nameGroup.body[i], \"textord\").text;\n    25→        }\n    26→\n    27→        if (funcName === \"\\\\begin\") {\n    28→            // begin...end is similar to left...right\n    29→            if (!environments.hasOwnProperty(envName)) {\n    30→                throw new ParseError(\n    31→                    \"No such environment: \" + envName, nameGroup);\n    32→            }\n    33→            // Build the environment object. Arguments and other information will\n    34→            // be made available to the begin and end methods using properties.\n    35→            const env = environments[envName];\n    36→            const {args, optArgs} =\n    37→                parser.parseArguments(\"\\\\begin{\" + envName + \"}\", env);\n    38→            const context = {\n    39→                mode: parser.mode,\n    40→                envName,\n    41→                parser,\n    42→            };\n    43→            const result = env.handler(context, args, optArgs);\n    44→            parser.expect(\"\\\\end\", false);\n    45→            const endNameToken = parser.nextToken;\n    46→            const end = assertNodeType(parser.parseFunction(), \"environment\");\n    47→            if (end.name !== envName) {\n    48→                throw new ParseError(\n    49→                    `Mismatch: \\\\begin{${envName}} matched by \\\\end{${end.name}}`,\n    50→                    endNameToken);\n    51→            }\n    52→            return result;\n    53→        }\n    54→\n    55→        return {\n    56→            type: \"environment\",\n    57→            mode: parser.mode,\n    58→            name: envName,\n    59→            nameGroup,\n    60→        };\n    61→    },\n    62→});\n    63→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it looks malicious. If it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:1"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "content: Nothing was found", "name": "Grep", "tool_call_id": "Grep:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Grep:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Grep", "arguments": "{\"-n\": true, \"output-mode\": \"content\", \"c\": 5, \"path\": \"/workspace/KaTeX/src/Parser.js\", \"pattern\": \"parseArguments.*function\"}"}}], "reasoning_content": "Perfect! Now I understand the flow:\n\n1. When `\\begin{matrix}` is encountered, the `\\begin` function handler is called\n2. Line 18: `const nameGroup = args[0];` - The `{matrix}` argument has already been parsed at this point\n3. Line 36-37: `parser.parseArguments` is called to parse any arguments for the environment (like `{cc}` for array)\n4. Line 43: `env.handler(context, args, optArgs)` - The environment handler (parseArray) is called\n5. In parseArray, line 65 beginGroup() is called\n6. Line 83: beginGroup() for first cell\n7. Line 95: `parser.parseExpression(false, \"\\\\cr\")` is called to parse the cell content\n\nThe issue is that `parser.parseArguments` on line 36-37 might be consuming tokens and executing macros BEFORE the cell groups are created in parseArray.\n\nLet me check how `parseArguments` works:"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"Text\": \"No matches found\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nThe TodoWrite tool hasn't been used recently. If you're working on tasks that would benefit from tracking progress, consider using the TodoWrite tool to track progress. Also consider cleaning up the todo list if has become stale and no longer matches what you are working on. Only use it if it's relevant to the current work. This is just a gentle reminder - ignore if not applicable.\\n\\n</system-reminder>\"}", "name": "G