e or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I found the issue! Looking at the code on lines 411-418, I can see that when the setter is called within a write-only atom's `write` function:\n\n1. If `a === atom` (same atom), it calls `setAtomValue` and `invalidateDependents` \n2. If `a !== atom` (different atom), it calls `writeAtomState` recursively\n\nThe problem is that when async operations are happening in write-only atoms, the `set` calls for other atoms (line 416: `writeAtomState(state, a, v)`) don't include the `flushPending(state)` call that would notify subscribers.\n\nHowever, looking more carefully at `writeAtomState`, I see that when it's called recursively for other atoms, if there are pending promises, the `flushPending` is only called when the promise resolves. But in our case, the async operations in the user's code are not returning promises from the `write` function—they're doing async work inside but not returning the promise.\n\nLet me look at how `setAtomValue` and `invalidateDependents` work:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Read:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/jotai/src/core/vanilla.ts\", \"limit\": 20, \"Offset\": 115}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "content": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" ?>\n<result>\n  <content_result>115→const setAtomValue = &lt;Value&gt;(\n   116→  state: State,\n   117→  atom: Atom&lt;Value&gt;,\n   118→  value: Value,\n   119→  dependencies?: Set&lt;AnyAtom&gt;,\n   120→  promise?: Promise&lt;void&gt;\n   121→): void =&gt; {\n   122→  const [atomState, prevDependencies] = wipAtomState(state, atom, dependencies)\n   123→  if (promise &amp;&amp; promise !== atomState?.p) {\n   124→    // newer async read is running, not updating\n   125→    return\n   126→  }\n   127→  delete atomState.e // read er