f it does, you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer high-level questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n", "name": "Read", "tool_call_id": "Read:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Now I understand the issue! The problem is in the async write operation flow:\n\n1. `commitAtomState` adds the atom to `state.p` (pending atoms)\n2. `flushPending` is supposed to notify all listeners of pending atoms\n3. But when async operations happen inside a write-only atom, the `set` calls don't immediately trigger `flushPending`\n\nThe key issue is that when we have async operations inside write-only atoms (like the user's `loadDocument` atom), the `set` calls happen asynchronously, but there's no `flushPending` call after those async `set` operations complete.\n\nLooking at the setter function (line 416), when `writeAtomState` is called for other atoms, it should trigger notifications, but the current implementation doesn't ensure `flushPending` is called after async operations in write-only atoms.\n\nLet me examine how the fix should work. The issue is in this pattern from the user's code:\n\n```js\nconst loadDocument = atom(\n  null,\n  (_get, set, documentId) => {\n    const fetch = async () => {\n      // async operations\n      set(loading, true)    // <- This set call doesn't trigger flushPending immediately\n      // ...\n      set(document, response)  // <- This set call doesn't trigger flushPending immediately  \n      set(loading, false)   // <- This set call doesn't trigger flushPending immediately\n    }\n    fetch() // <- The async function is called but not awaited\n  }\n)\n```\n\nThe problem is that the `set` calls happen inside an async function, but since the atom's `write` function doesn't return a promise, Jotai doesn't know to wait for and flush pending state updates.\n\nThe fix should ensure that whenever `setAtomValue` is called (which adds atoms to the pending state), we eventually call `flushPending` to notify subscribers.\n\nLooking at the setter implementation, I can see that when we call `writeAtomState(state, a, v)` for other atoms from within a write-only atom, we need to ensure that `flushPending` gets called.\n\nLet me examine the fix. The issue is in the setter function in `writeAtomState`:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "Edit:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "Edit", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"/workspace/jotai/src/core/vanilla.ts\", \"old_string\": \"      ((a: AnyWritableAtom, v: unknown) => {\\n        if (a === atom) {\\n          setAtomValue(state, a, v)\\n          invalidateDependents(state, a)\\n        } else {\\n          writeAtomState(state, a, v)\\n        }\\n      }) as Setter,\", \"new_string\": \"      ((a: AnyWritableAtom, v: unknown) => {\\n        if (a === atom) {\\n          setAtomValue(state, a, v)\\n          invalidateDependents(state, a)\\n        } else {\\n          writeAtomState(state, a, v)\\n        }\\n        // Ensure pending atoms are flushed after synchronous writes\\n        // This handles the case where async operations call set() but don't return a promise\\n        if (state.p.size > 0) {\\n          Promise.resolve().then(() => flushPending(state))\\n        }\\n      }) as Setter,\"}"}}]}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"TEXTRESULT\": \"The file /workspace/jotai/src/core/vanilla.ts has been updated. Here's the result of running `cat -n` on a snippet of the edited file:\\n   407→          )\\n   408→        }\\n   409→        throw new Error('no value found')\\n   410→      }) as Getter,\\n   411→      ((a: AnyWritableAtom, v: unknown) => {\\n   412→        if (a === atom) {\\n   413→          setAtomValue(state, a, v)\\n   414→          invalidateDependents(state, a)\\n   415→        } else {\\n   416→          writeAtomState(state, a, v)\\n   417→        }\\n   418→        // Ensure pending atoms are flushed after synchronous writes\\n   419→        // This handles the case where async operations call set() but don't return a promise\\n   420→        if (state.p.size > 0) {\\n   421→          Promise.resolve().then(() => flushPending(state))\\n   422→        }\\n   423→      }) as Setter,\\n   424→      update\\n   425→    )\\n   426→    if (promiseOrVoid instanceof Promise) {\\n   427→      if (pendingPromises) {\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nThe TodoWrite tool hasn't been used recently. If you're working on tasks that would benefit from tracking progress, consider using the TodoWrite tool to track progress. Also consider cleaning up the todo list if has become stale and no longer matches what you are working on. Only use it if it's relevant to the current work. This is just a gentle reminder - ignore if not applicable.\\n\\n</system-reminder>\"}", "name": "Edit", "tool_call_id": "Edit:0"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Now let me test the fix by running the reproduction test:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "TodoWrite:0", "type": "function", "function": {"name": "TodoWrite", "arguments": "{\"todos\": [{\"activeForm\": \"Exploring the repository structure\", \"content\": \"Explore the repository structure to understand Jotai\", \"status\": \"completed\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Creating a reproduction script\", \"content\": \"Create a reproduction script to confirm the issue\", \"status\": \"completed\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Analyzing the problem in source code\", \"content\": \"Analyze the problem in the Jotai source code\", \"status\": \"completed\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Implementing the fix\", \"content\": \"Implement the fix for the atom update issue\", \"status\": \"completed\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Testing the fix\", \"content\": \"Test the fix with the reproduction script\", \"status\": \"in_progress\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Running existing tests\", \"content\": \"Run existing tests to ensure no regressions\", \"status\": \"pending\"}, {\"activeForm\": \"Cleaning up temporary files\", \"content\": \"Clean up temporary files\", \"status\": \