0-595 ...\n   630 ... eliding lines 630-640 ...\n   646 ... eliding lines 646-663 ...\n   667 ... eliding lines 667-671 ...\n   675 ... eliding lines 675-678 ...\n   682 ... eliding lines 682-685 ...", "name": "file_editor", "tool_call_id": "call_function_3615870302_1"}, {"content": "Let me examine the `get_prefetch_queryset` method more closely, as this is likely where the issue with UUID primary keys occurs:\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_3597081955_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/testbed/django/contrib/contenttypes/fields.py\", \"view_range\": [171, 215]}", "name": "file_editor"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "This file is quite large. Let me focus on the `get_prefetch_queryset` method of the `GenericForeignKey` class since that's likely where the issue lies. The prefetch_related functionality depends on this method to work properly."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Execution output of [file_editor]:\nHere's the result of running `cat -n` on the file: /testbed/django/contrib/contenttypes/fields.py:\n   171     def get_prefetch_queryset(self, instances, queryset=None):\n   172         if queryset is not None:\n   173             raise ValueError(\"Custom queryset can't be used for this lookup.\")\n   174 \n   175         # For efficiency, group the instances by content type and then do one\n   176         # query per model\n   177         fk_dict = defaultdict(set)\n   178         # We need one instance for each group in order to get the right db:\n   179         instance_dict = {}\n   180         ct_attname = self.model._meta.get_field(self.ct_field).get_attname()\n   181         for instance in instances:\n   182             # We avoid looking for values if either ct_id or fkey value is None\n   183             ct_id = getattr(instance, ct_attname)\n   184             if ct_id is not None:\n   185                 fk_val = getattr(instance, self.fk_field)\n   186                 if fk_val is not None:\n   187                     fk_dict[ct_id].add(fk_val)\n   188                     instance_dict[ct_id] = instance\n   189 \n   190         ret_val = []\n   191         for ct_id, fkeys in fk_dict.items():\n   192             instance = instance_dict[ct_id]\n   193             ct = self.get_content_type(id=ct_id, using=instance._state.db)\n   194             ret_val.extend(ct.get_all_objects_for_this_type(pk__in=fkeys))\n   195 \n   196         # For doing the join in Python, we have to match both the FK val and the\n   197         # content type, so we use a callable that returns a (fk, class) pair.\n   198         def gfk_key(obj):\n   199             ct_id = getattr(obj, ct_attname)\n   200             if ct_id is None:\n   201                 return None\n   202             else:\n   203                 model = self.get_content_type(id=ct_id,\n   204                                               using=obj._state.db).model_class()\n   205                 return (model._meta.pk.get_prep_value(getattr(obj, self.fk_field)),\n   206                         model)\n   207 \n   208         return (\n   209             ret_val,\n   210             lambda obj: (obj.pk, obj.__class__),\n   211             gfk_key,\n   212             True,\n   213             self.name,\n   214             True,\n   215         )", "name": "file_editor", "tool_call_id": "call_function_3597081955_1"}, {"content": "\nLet me check the existing tests for generic relations to understand the expected behavior:\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_4469389437_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/testbed/tests/generic_relations\"}", "name": "file_editor"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "Now! I can see the issue. In the `gfk_key` function on line 205, there's a call to `model._meta.pk.get_prep_value(getattr(obj, self.fk_field))`. This is used to prepare the foreign key value for matching.\n\nThe problem is that when the primary key is a UUID field, the `get_prep_value` method might be converting the UUID to a string or some other format, but the `fk_val` that's stored in the database might still be in its original format.\n\nLet me look at the tests to understand the expected behavior and see if there are any existing tests for UUID primary keys."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Execution output of [file_editor]:\nHere's the files and directories up to 2 levels deep in /testbed/tests/generic_relations, excluding hidden:\n/testbed/tests/generic_relations\n/testbed/tests/generic_relations/tests.py\n/testbed/tests/generic_relations/test_forms.py\n/testbed/tests/generic_relations/models.py\n/testbed/tests/generic_relations/__init__.py\n", "name": "file_editor", "tool_call_id": "call_function_4469389437_1"}, {"content": "\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_9600812360_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/testbed/tests/generic_relations/models.py\"}", "name": "file_editor"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "Let me look at the generic relations tests to understand the expected behavior and see if there are any tests for UUID primary keys."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Execution output of [file_editor]:\nHere is a condensed view for file: /testbed/tests/generic_relations/models.py; [Note: Useful for understanding file structure in a concise manner. Please use specific view_range without concise cmd if you want to explore further into the relevant parts.]\n     1 \"\"\"\n     2 Generic relations\n     3 \n     4 Generic relations let an object have a foreign key to any object through a\n     5 content-type/object-id field. A ``GenericForeignKey`` field can point to any\n     6 object, be it animal, vegetable, or mineral.\n     7 \n     8 The canonical example is tags (although this example implementation is *far*\n     9 from complete).\n    10 \"\"\"\n    11 \n    12 from django.contrib.contenttypes.fields import (\n    13     GenericForeignKey, GenericRelation,\n    14 )\n    15 from django.contrib.contenttypes.models import ContentType\n    16 from django.db import models\n    17 \n    18 \n    19 class TaggedItem(models.Model):\n    20     \"\"\"A tag on an item.\"\"\"\n    21     tag = models.SlugField()\n    22     content_type = models.ForeignKey(ContentType, models.CASCADE)\n    23     object_id = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n    24 \n    25     content_object = GenericForeignKey()\n    26 \n    27     class Meta:\n    28         ordering = [\"tag\", \"content_type__model\"]\n    29 \n    30     def __str__(self):\n    31         return self.tag\n    32 \n    33 \n    34 class ValuableTaggedItem(TaggedItem):\n    35     value = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n    36 \n    37 \n    38 class AbstractComparison(models.Model):\n    39     comparative = models.CharField(max_length=50)\n    40 \n    41     content_type1 = models.ForeignKey(ContentType, models.CASCADE, related_name=\"comparative1_set\")\n    42     object_id1 = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n    43 \n    44     first_obj = GenericForeignKey(ct_field=\"content_type1\", fk_field=\"object_id1\")\n    45 \n    46 \n    47 class Comparison(AbstractComparison):\n    48     \"\"\"\n    49     A model that tests having multiple GenericForeignKeys. One is defined\n    50     through an inherited abstract model and one defined directly on this class.\n    51     \"\"\"\n    52     content_type2 = models.ForeignKey(ContentType, models.CASCADE, related_name=\"comparative2_set\")\n    53     object_id2 = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n    54 \n    55     other_obj = GenericForeignKey(ct_field=\"content_type2\", fk_field=\"object_id2\")\n    56 \n    57     def __str__(self):\n    58         return \"%s is %s than %s\" % (self.first_obj, self.comparative, self.other_obj)\n    59 \n    60 \n    61 class Animal(models.Model):\n    62     common_name = models.CharField(max_length=150)\n    63     latin_name = models.CharField(max_length=150)\n    64 \n    65     tags = GenericRelation(TaggedItem, related_query_name='animal')\n    66     comparisons = GenericRelation(Comparison,\n    67                                   object_id_field=\"object_id1\",\n    68                                   content_type_field=\"content_type1\")\n    69 \n    70     def __str__(self):\n    71         return self.common_name\n    72 \n    73 \n    74 class Vegetable(models.Model):\n    75     name = models.CharField(max_length=150)\n    76     is_yucky = models.BooleanField(default=True)\n    77 \n    78     tags = GenericRelation(TaggedItem)\n    79 \n    80     def __str__(self):\n    81         return self.name\n    82 \n    83 \n    84 class Carrot(Vegetable):\n    85     pass\n    86 \n    87 \n    88 class Mineral(models.Model):\n    89     name = models.CharField(max_length=150)\n    90     hardness = models.PositiveSmallIntegerField()\n    91 \n    92     # note the lack of an explicit GenericRelation here...\n    93 \n    94     def __str__(self):\n    95         return self.name\n    96 \n    97 \n    98 class GeckoManager(models.Manager):\n    99     def get_queryset(self):\n   100         return super().get_queryset().filter(has_tail=True)\n   101 \n   102 \n   103 class Gecko(models.Model):\n   104     has_tail = models.BooleanField(default=False)\n   105     objects = GeckoManager()\n   106 \n   107 \n   108 # To test fix for #11263\n   109 class Rock(Mineral):\n   110     tags = GenericRelation(TaggedItem)\n   111 \n   112 \n   113 class ValuableRock(Mineral):\n   114     tags = GenericRelation(ValuableTaggedItem)\n   115 \n   116 \n   117 class ManualPK(models.Model):\n   118     id = models.IntegerField(primary_key=True)\n   119     tags = GenericRelation(TaggedItem, related_query_name='manualpk')\n   120 \n   121 \n   122 class ForProxyModelModel(models.Model):\n   123     content_type = models.ForeignKey(ContentType, models.CASCADE)\n   124     object_id = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n   125     obj = GenericForeignKey(for_concrete_model=False)\n   126     title = models.CharField(max_length=255, null=True)\n   127 \n   128 \n   129 class ForConcreteModelModel(models.Model):\n   130     content_type = models.ForeignKey(ContentType, models.CASCADE)\n   131     object_id = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n   132     obj = GenericForeignKey()\n   133 \n   134 \n   135 class ConcreteRelatedModel(models.Model):\n   136     bases = GenericRelation(ForProxyModelModel, for_concrete_model=False)\n   137 \n   138 \n   139 class ProxyRelatedModel(ConcreteRelatedModel):\n   140     class Meta:\n   141         proxy = True\n   142 \n   143 \n   144 # To test fix for #7551\n   145 class AllowsNullGFK(models.Model):\n   146     content_type = models.ForeignKey(ContentType, models.SET_NULL, null=True)\n   147     object_id = models.PositiveIntegerField(null=True)\n   148     content_object = GenericForeignKey()", "name": "file_editor", "tool_call_id": "call_function_9600812360_1"}, {"content": "\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_7886763087_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/testbed/tests/prefetch_related\"}", "name": "file_editor"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "I can see that the existing models all use `PositiveIntegerField` for the `object_id` field in the GenericForeignKey. This is the standard setup, but the issue is with UUID primary keys.\n\nLet me also check the prefetch_related tests to see if there are any tests for generic relations with prefetch_related."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Execution output of [file_editor]:\nHere's the files and directories up to 2 levels deep in /testbed/tests/prefetch_related, excluding hidden:\n/testbed/tests/prefetch_related\n/testbed/tests/prefetch_related/test_uuid.py\n/testbed/tests/prefetch_related/tests.py\n/testbed/tests/prefetch_related/models.py\n/testbed/tests/prefetch_related/__init__.py\n/testbed/tests/prefetch_related/test_prefetch_related_objects.py\n", "name": "file_editor", "tool_call_id": "call_function_7886763087_1"}, {"content": "\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_9635898101_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/testbed/tests/prefetch_related/test_uuid.py\"}", "name": "file_editor"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "Interesting! There's a `test_uuid.py` file in the prefetch_related tests. This might be directly relevant to our issue. Let me examine it."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Execution output of [file_editor]:\nHere's the result of running `cat -n` on the file: /testbed/tests/prefetch_related/test_uuid.py:\n     1 from django.test import TestCase\n     2 \n     3 from .models import Flea, House, Person, Pet, Room\n     4 \n     5 \n     6 class UUIDPrefetchRelated(TestCase):\n     7 \n     8     def test_prefetch_related_from_uuid_model(self):\n     9         Pet.objects.create(name='Fifi').people.add(\n    10             Person.objects.create(name='Ellen'),\n    11             Person.objects.create(name='George'),\n    12         )\n    13 \n    14         with self.assertNumQueries(2):\n    15             pet = Pet.objects.prefetch_related('people').get(name='Fifi')\n    16         with self.assertNumQueries(0):\n    17             self.assertEqual(2, len(pet.people.all()))\n    18 \n    19     def test_prefetch_related_to_uuid_model(self):\n    20         Person.objects.create(name='Bella').pets.add(\n    21             Pet.objects.create(name='Socks'),\n    22             Pet.objects.create(name='Coffee'),\n    23         )\n    24 \n    25         with self.assertNumQueries(2):\n    26             person = Person.objects.prefetch_related('pets').get(name='Bella')\n    27         with self.assertNumQueries(0):\n    28             self.assertEqual(2, len(person.pets.all()))\n    29 \n    30     def test_prefetch_related_from_uuid_model_to_uuid_model(self):\n    31         fleas = [Flea.objects.create() for i in range(3)]\n    32         Pet.objects.create(name='Fifi').fleas_hosted.add(*fleas)\n    33         Pet.objects.create(name='Bobo').fleas_hosted.add(*fleas)\n    34 \n    35         with self.assertNumQueries(2):\n    36             pet = Pet.objects.prefetch_related('fleas_hosted').get(name='Fifi')\n    37         with self.assertNumQueries(0):\n    38             self.assertEqual(3, len(pet.fleas_hosted.all()))\n    39 \n    40         with self.assertNumQueries(2):\n    41             flea = Flea.objects.prefetch_related('pets_visited').get(pk=fleas[0].pk)\n    42         with self.assertNumQueries(0):\n    43             self.assertEqual(2, len(flea.pets_visited.all()))\n    44 \n    45     def test_prefetch_related_from_uuid_model_to_uuid_model_with_values_flat(self):\n    46         pet = Pet.objects.create(name='Fifi')\n    47         pet.people.add(\n    48             Person.objects.create(name='Ellen'),\n    49             Person.objects.create(name='George'),\n    50         )\n    51         self.assertSequenceEqual(\n    52             Pet.objects.prefetch_related('fleas_hosted').values_list('id', flat=True),\n    53             [pet.id]\n    54         )\n    55 \n    56 \n    57 class UUIDPrefetchRelatedLookups(TestCase):\n    58 \n    59     @classmethod\n    60     def setUpTestData(cls):\n    61         house = House.objects.create(name='Redwood', address='Arcata')\n    62         room = Room.objects.create(name='Racoon', house=house)\n    63         fleas = [Flea.objects.create(current_room=room) for i in range(3)]\n    64         pet = Pet.objects.create(name='Spooky')\n    65         pet.fleas_hosted.add(*fleas)\n    66         person = Person.objects.create(name='Bob')\n    67         person.houses.add(house)\n    68         person.pets.add(pet)\n    69         person.fleas_hosted.add(*fleas)\n    70 \n    71     def test_from_uuid_pk_lookup_uuid_pk_integer_pk(self):\n    72         # From uuid-pk model, prefetch <uuid-pk model>.<integer-pk model>:\n    73         with self.assertNumQueries(4):\n    74             spooky = Pet.objects.prefetch_related('fleas_hosted__current_room__house').get(name='Spooky')\n    75         with self.assertNumQueries(0):\n    76             self.assertEqual('Racoon', spooky.fleas_hosted.all()[0].current_room.name)\n    77 \n    78     def test_from_uuid_pk_lookup_integer_pk2_uuid_pk2(self):\n    79         # From uuid-pk model, prefetch <integer-pk model>.<integer-pk model>.<uuid-pk model>.<uuid-pk model>:\n    80         with self.assertNumQueries(5):\n    81             spooky = Pet.objects.prefetch_related('people__houses__rooms__fleas').get(name='Spooky')\n    82         with self.assertNumQueries(0):\n    83             self.assertEqual(3, len(spooky.people.all()[0].houses.all()[0].rooms.all()[0].fleas.all()))\n    84 \n    85     def test_from_integer_pk_lookup_uuid_pk_integer_pk(self):\n    86         # From integer-pk model, prefetch <uuid-pk model>.<integer-pk model>:\n    87         with self.assertNumQueries(3):\n    88             racoon = Room.objects.prefetch_related('fleas__people_visited').get(name='Racoon')\n    89         with self.assertNumQueries(0):\n    90             self.assertEqual('Bob', racoon.fleas.all()[0].people_visited.all()[0].name)\n    91 \n    92     def test_from_integer_pk_lookup_integer_pk_uuid_pk(self):\n    93         # From integer-pk model, prefetch <integer-pk model>.<uuid-pk model>:\n    94         with self.assertNumQueries(3):\n    95             redwood = House.objects.prefetch_related('rooms__fleas').get(name='Redwood')\n    96         with self.assertNumQueries(0):\n    97             self.assertEqual(3, len(redwood.rooms.all()[0].fleas.all()))\n    98 \n    99     def test_from_integer_pk_lookup_integer_pk_uuid_pk_uuid_pk(self):\n   100         # From integer-pk model, prefetch <integer-pk model>.<uuid-pk model>.<uuid-pk model>:\n   101         with self.assertNumQueries(4):\n   102             redwood = House.objects.prefetch_related('rooms__fleas__pets_visited').get(name='Redwood')\n   103         with self.assertNumQueries(0):\n   104             self.assertEqual('Spooky', redwood.rooms.all()[0].fleas.all()[0].pets_visited.all()[0].name)", "name": "file_editor", "tool_call_id": "call_function_9635898101_1"}, {"content": "\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_4794251472_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/testbed/tests/prefetch_related/models.py\"}", "name": "file_editor"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "Great! This test file shows that UUID prefetch_related is tested, but I don't see any tests specifically for GenericForeignKey with UUID primary keys. Let me look at the models used in these tests to understand the structure."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Execution output of [file_editor]:\nHere is a condensed view for file: /testbed/tests/prefetch_related/models.py; [Note: Useful for understanding file structure in a concise manner. Please use specific view_range without concise cmd if you want to explore further into the relevant parts.]\n     1 import uuid\n     2 \n     3 from django.contrib.contenttypes.fields import (\n     4     GenericForeignKey, GenericRelation,\n     5 )\n     6 from django.contrib.contenttypes.models import ContentType\n     7 from django.db import models\n     8 from django.db.models.query import ModelIterable, QuerySet\n     9 from django.utils.functional import cached_property\n    10 \n    11 \n    12 class Author(models.Model):\n    13     name = models.CharField(max_length=50, unique=True)\n    14     first_book = models.ForeignKey('Book', models.CASCADE, related_name='first_time_authors')\n    15     favorite_authors = models.ManyToManyField(\n    16         'self', through='FavoriteAuthors', symmetrical=False, related_name='favors_me')\n    17 \n    18     class Meta:\n    19         ordering = ['id']\n    20 \n    21     def __str__(self):\n    22         return self.name\n    23 \n    24 \n    25 class AuthorWithAge(Author):\n    26     author = models.OneToOneField(Author, models.CASCADE, parent_link=True)\n    27     age = models.IntegerField()\n    28 \n    29 \n    30 class FavoriteAuthors(models.Model):\n    31     author = models.ForeignKey(Author, models.CASCADE, to_field='name', related_name='i_like')\n    32     likes_author = models.ForeignKey(Author, models.CASCADE, to_field='name', related_name='likes_me')\n    33 \n    34     class Meta:\n    35         ordering = ['id']\n    36 \n    37 \n    38 class AuthorAddress(models.Model):\n    39     author = models.ForeignKey(Author, models.CASCADE, to_field='name', related_name='addresses')\n    40     address = models.TextField()\n    41 \n    42     class Meta:\n    43         ordering = ['id']\n    44 \n    45     def __str__(self):\n    46         return self.address\n    47 \n    48 \n    49 class Book(models.Model):\n    50     title = models.CharField(max_length=255)\n    51     authors = models.ManyToManyField(Author, related_name='books')\n    52 \n    53     class Meta:\n    54         ordering = ['id']\n    55 \n    56     def __str__(self):\n    57         return self.title\n    58 \n    59 \n    60 class BookWithYear(Book):\n    61     book = models.OneToOneField(Book, models.CASCADE, parent_link=True)\n    62     published_year = models.IntegerField()\n    63     aged_authors = models.ManyToManyField(\n    64         AuthorWithAge, related_name='books_with_year')\n    65 \n    66 \n    67 class Bio(models.Model):\n    68     author = models.OneToOneField(\n    69         Author,\n    70         models.CASCADE,\n    71         primary_key=True,\n    72         to_field='name',\n    73     )\n    74     books = models.ManyToManyField(Book, blank=True)\n    75 \n    76 \n    77 class Reader(models.Model):\n    78     name = models.CharField(max_length=50)\n    79     books_read = models.ManyToManyField(Book, related_name='read_by')\n    80 \n    81     class Meta:\n    82         ordering = ['id']\n    83 \n    84     def __str__(self):\n    85         return self.name\n    86 \n    87 \n    88 class BookReview(models.Model):\n    89     # Intentionally does not have a related name.\n    90     book = models.ForeignKey(BookWithYear, models.CASCADE, null=True)\n    91     notes = models.TextField(null=True, blank=True)\n    92 \n    93 \n    94 # Models for default manager tests\n    95 \n    96 class Qualification(models.Model):\n    97     name = models.CharField(max_length=10)\n    98 \n    99     class Meta:\n   100         ordering = ['id']\n   101 \n   102 \n   103 class ModelIterableSubclass(ModelIterable):\n   104     pass\n   105 \n   106 \n   107 class TeacherQuerySet(QuerySet):\n   108     def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n   109         super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\n   110         self._iterable_class = ModelIterableSubclass\n   111 \n   112 \n   113 class TeacherManager(models.Manager):\n   114     def get_queryset(self):\n   115         return super().get_queryset().prefetch_related('qualifications')\n   116 \n   117 \n   118 class Teacher(models.Model):\n   119     name = models.CharField(max_length=50)\n   120     qualifications = models.ManyToManyField(Qualification)\n   121 \n   122     objects = TeacherManager()\n   123     objects_custom = TeacherQuerySet.as_manager()\n   124 \n   125     class Meta:\n   126         ordering = ['id']\n   127 \n   128     def __str__(self):\n   129         return \"%s (%s)\" % (self.name, \", \".join(q.name for q in self.qualifications.all()))\n   130 \n   131 \n   132 class Department(models.Model):\n   133     name = models.CharField(max_length=50)\n   134     teachers = models.ManyToManyField(Teacher)\n   135 \n   136     class Meta:\n   137         ordering = ['id']\n   138 \n   139 \n   140 # GenericRelation/GenericForeignKey tests\n   141 \n   142 class TaggedItem(models.Model):\n   143     tag = models.SlugField()\n   144     content_type = models.ForeignKey(\n   145         ContentType,\n   146         models.CASCADE,\n   147         related_name=\"taggeditem_set2\",\n   148     )\n   149     object_id = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n   150     content_object = GenericForeignKey('content_type', 'object_id')\n   151     created_by_ct = models.ForeignKey(\n   152         ContentType,\n   153         models.SET_NULL,\n   154         null=True,\n   155         related_name='taggeditem_set3',\n   156     )\n   157     created_by_fkey = models.PositiveIntegerField(null=True)\n   158     created_by = GenericForeignKey('created_by_ct', 'created_by_fkey',)\n   159     favorite_ct = models.ForeignKey(\n   160         ContentType,\n   161         models.SET_NULL,\n   162         null=True,\n   163         related_name='taggeditem_set4',\n   164     )\n   165     favorite_fkey = models.CharField(max_length=64, null=True)\n   166     favorite = GenericForeignKey('favorite_ct', 'favorite_fkey')\n   167 \n   168     class Meta:\n   169         ordering = ['id']\n   170 \n   171     def __str__(self):\n   172         return self.tag\n   173 \n   174 \n   175 class Bookmark(models.Model):\n   176     url = models.URLField()\n   177     tags = GenericRelation(TaggedItem, related_query_name='bookmarks')\n   178     favorite_tags = GenericRelation(TaggedItem,\n   179                                     content_type_field='favorite_ct',\n   180                                     object_id_field='favorite_fkey',\n   181                                     related_query_name='favorite_bookmarks')\n   182 \n   183     class Meta:\n   184         ordering = ['id']\n   185 \n   186 \n   187 class Comment(models.Model):\n   188     comment = models.TextField()\n   189 \n   190     # Content-object field\n   191     content_type = models.ForeignKey(ContentType, models.CASCADE)\n   192     object_pk = models.TextField()\n   193     content_object = GenericForeignKey(ct_field=\"content_type\", fk_field=\"object_pk\")\n   194 \n   195     class Meta:\n   196         ordering = ['id']\n   197 \n   198 \n   199 # Models for lookup ordering tests\n   200 \n   201 class House(models.Model):\n   202     name = models.CharField(max_length=50)\n   203     address = models.CharField(max_length=255)\n   204     owner = models.ForeignKey('Person', models.SET_NULL, null=True)\n   205     main_room = models.OneToOneField('Room', models.SET_NULL, related_name='main_room_of', null=True)\n   206 \n   207     class Meta:\n   208         ordering = ['id']\n   209 \n   210 \n   211 class Room(models.Model):\n   212     name = models.CharField(max_length=50)\n   213     house = models.ForeignKey(House, models.CASCADE, related_name='rooms')\n   214 \n   215     class Meta:\n   216         ordering = ['id']\n   217 \n   218 \n   219 class Person(models.Model):\n   220     name = models.CharField(max_length=50)\n   221     houses = models.ManyToManyField(House, related_name='occupants')\n   222 \n   223     @property\n   224     def primary_house(self):\n   225         # Assume business logic forces every person to have at least one house.\n   226         return sorted(self.houses.all(), key=lambda house: -house.rooms.count())[0]\n   227 \n   228     @property\n   229     def all_houses(self):\n   230         return list(self.houses.all())\n   231 \n   232     @cached_property\n   233     def cached_all_houses(self):\n   234         return self.all_houses\n   235 \n   236     class Meta:\n   237         ordering = ['id']\n   238 \n   239 \n   240 # Models for nullable FK tests\n   241 \n   242 class Employee(models.Model):\n   243     name = models.CharField(max_length=50)\n   244     boss = models.ForeignKey('self', models.SET_NULL, null=True, related_name='serfs')\n   245 \n   246     class Meta:\n   247         ordering = ['id']\n   248 \n   249     def __str__(self):\n   250         return self.name\n   251 \n   252 \n   253 # Ticket #19607\n   254 \n   255 class LessonEntry(models.Model):\n   256     name1 = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n   257     name2 = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n   258 \n   259     def __str__(self):\n   260         return \"%s %s\" % (self.name1, self.name2)\n   261 \n   262 \n   263 class WordEntry(models.Model):\n   264     lesson_entry = models.ForeignKey(LessonEntry, models.CASCADE)\n   265     name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n   266 \n   267     def __str__(self):\n   268         return \"%s (%s)\" % (self.name, self.id)\n   269 \n   270 \n   271 # Ticket #21410: Regression when related_name=\"+\"\n   272 \n   273 class Author2(models.Model):\n   274     name = models.CharField(max_length=50, unique=True)\n   275     first_book = models.ForeignKey('Book', models.CASCADE, related_name='first_time_authors+')\n   276     favorite_books = models.ManyToManyField('Book', related_name='+')\n   277 \n   278     class Meta:\n   279         ordering = ['id']\n   280 \n   281     def __str__(self):\n   282         return self.name\n   283 \n   284 \n   285 # Models for many-to-many with UUID pk test:\n   286 \n   287 class Pet(models.Model):\n   288     id = models.UUIDField(primary_key=True, default=uuid.uuid4, editable=False)\n   <response clipped><NOTE>To save on context only part of this file has been shown to you. You should retry this tool after you have searched inside the file with `grep -n` in order to find the line numbers of what you are looking for.</NOTE>", "name": "file_editor", "tool_call_id": "call_function_4794251472_1"}, {"content": "\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_2897631126_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"search_term\": \"class.*UUIDField.*primary_key\", \"path\": \"/testbed/tests/prefetch_related/models.py\"}", "name": "search"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "Great! I can see that there are models with UUID primary keys in this file. Let me look at the specific UUID models to understand the structure better."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Execution output of [search]:\nNo matches found for \"class.*UUIDField.*primary_key\" in /testbed/tests/prefetch_related/models.py", "name": "search", "tool_call_id": "call_function_2897631126_1"}, {"content": "\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_6343264047_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"path\": \"/testbed/tests/prefetch_related/models.py\", \"search_term\": \"UUIDField\"}", "name": "search"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "Let me search for the UUID models more specifically."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Execution output of [search]:\nMatches for \"UUIDField\" in /testbed/tests/prefetch_related/models.py:\n288:    id = models.UUIDField(primary_key=True, default=uuid.uuid4, editable=False)\n294:    id = models.UUIDField(primary_key=True, default=uuid.uuid4, editable=False)", "name": "search", "tool_call_id": "call_function_6343264047_1"}, {"content": "\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_6981260431_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/testbed/tests/prefetch_related/models.py\", \"view_range\": [285, 310]}", "name": "file_editor"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "Now! Let me look at those specific models to understand the structure."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Execution output of [file_editor]:\nERROR: Invalid view_range [285, 310]: end must be >= start and <= 297, or -1 to view until end.", "name": "file_editor", "tool_call_id": "call_function_6981260431_1"}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "file_editor", "description": "Custom editing tool for viewing, creating and editing files\n* State is persistent across command calls and discussions with the user\n* If `path` is a file, `view` displays the result of applying `cat -n`. If `path` is a directory, `view` lists non-hidden files and directories up to 2 levels deep\n* The `create` command cannot be used if the specified `path` already exists as a file\n* If a `command` generates a long output, it will be truncated and marked with `<response clipped>`\n* The `undo_edit` command will revert the last edit made to the file at `path`\n\nNotes for using the `str_replace` command:\n* The `old_str` parameter should match EXACTLY one or more consecutive lines from the original file. Be mindful of whitespaces!\n* If the `old_str` parameter is not unique in the file, the replacement will not be performed. Make sure to include enough context in `old_str` to make it unique\n* The `new_str` parameter should contain the edited lines that should replace the `old_str`\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"command": {"description": "The command to run. Allowed options are: `view`, `create`, `str_replace`, `insert`, `undo_edit`.", "enum": ["view", "create", "str_replace", "insert", "undo_edit"], "type": "string"}, "path": {"description": "Absolute path to file or directory, e.g. `/testbed/file.py` or `/testbed`.", "type": "string"}, "file_text": {"description": "Required for the `create` command, contains the content of the file to be created.", "type": "string"}, "old_str": {"description": "Required for the `str_replace` command, specifies the string in `path` to replace.", "type": "string"}, "new_str": {"description": "Optional for the `str_replace` command to specify the replacement string. Required for the `insert` command to specify the string to insert.", "type": "string"}, "insert_line": {"description": "Required for the `insert` command. The `new_str` will be inserted AFTER the line specified.", "type": "integer"}, "view_range": {"description": "Optional for the `view` command when `path` points to a file. Specifies the line range to view. E.g., [11, 12] shows lines 11 and 12. Indexing starts at 1. Use [start_line, -1] to show all lines from `start_line` to the end.", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}}, "concise": {"description": "Optional for the `view` command. If `True`, displays a concise skeletal view of the file. Very useful for localization tasks. Highly recommended for large files.", "type": "boolean"}}, "required": ["command", "path"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "execute_bash", "description": "\nDescription: Execute a bash command in the terminal.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) command (string, required): The bash command to execute. For example: `python my_script.py`\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"cmd": {"type": "string", "description": "The command (and optional arguments) to execute. For example: 'python my_script.py'"}}, "required": ["cmd"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search", "description": "\nDescription: Search for a term in either a directory or a single file.\n\nBehavior:\n* If `--path` points to a directory (default is `.`), we recursively search all non-hidden files and directories.\n* If `--path` points to a file, we run `grep -n` on that file to find line numbers containing the search term.\n* If more than 100 files match (directory search scenario), the tool will stop listing and inform you to narrow your search.\n* If no files are found that match your search term, the tool will inform you of that as well.\n\n**Parameters:**\n  1. **search_term** (`string`, required): The term to search for in files.\n  2. **path** (`string`, optional): The file or directory in which to search. If not provided, defaults to the current directory (i.e., `.`).\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_term": {"description": "The term to search for in files.", "type": "string"}, "path": {"description": "The file or directory to search in. Defaults to `.` if not specified.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["search_term"]}}}], "model": "minimax-m2.5"}, "response": {"id": "0645005b8aa44d6a510993bf47773f3e", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "tool_calls", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "<think>\nLet me check the length of the file.\n</think>\n\n\n", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_6fx132zed5wy_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"cmd\": \"wc -l /testbed/tests/prefetch_related/models.py\"}", "name": "execute_bash"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "name": "MiniMax AI", "audio_content": ""}}], "created": 1777716571, "model": "MiniMax-M2.5", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 49, "prompt_tokens": 16671, "total_tokens": 16720, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 13, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 16571}, "total_characters": 0}, "input_sensitive": false, "output_sensitive": false, "input_sensitive_type": 0, "output_sensitive_type": 0, "output_sensitive_int": 0, "base_resp": {"status_code": 0, "status_msg": ""}}, "status": "success", "finish_reason": "tool_calls", "last_run_at": "2026-05-02T18:09:32.353896", "duration_ms": 8669, "hash": "b811f7fe4e4d03e59bedc57178cebe59", "provider": "", "expected_tool_call": true, "error_only_reasoning_checked": 1, "error_only_reasoning": false, "tool_calls_finish_reason": "tool_calls", "tool_calls_valid": true, "tool_calls_count": 1}
{"data_index": 54, "request": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that can interact with a computer to solve tasks. You are provided with a local git repository and a GitHub issue. Your goal is to identify, implement, and verify a solution to the issue while documenting your reasoning process. You have access to tools for viewing and editing files, executing bash commands, and submitting your final solution."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I have uploaded a python code repository in the /testbed directory.\n\nNow consider the following Github issue:\n\n<github_issue>\nMake URLValidator reject invalid characters in the username and password\nDescription\n\t \n\t\t(last modified by Tim Bell)\n\t \nSince #20003, core.validators.URLValidator accepts URLs with usernames and passwords. RFC 1738 section 3.1 requires \"Within the user and password field, any \":\", \"@\", or \"/\" must be encoded\"; however, those characters are currently accepted without being %-encoded. That allows certain invalid URLs to pass validation incorrectly. (The issue originates in Diego Perini's ​gist, from which the implementation in #20003 was derived.)\nAn example URL that should be invalid is http://foo/bar@example.com; furthermore, many of the test cases in tests/validators/invalid_urls.txt would be rendered valid under the current implementation by appending a query string of the form ?m=foo@example.com to them.\nI note Tim Graham's concern about adding complexity to the validation regex. However, I take the opposite position to Danilo Bargen about invalid URL edge cases: it's not fine if invalid URLs (even so-called \"edge cases\") are accepted when the regex could be fixed simply to reject them correctly. I also note that a URL of the form above was encountered in a production setting, so that this is a genuine use case, not merely an academic exercise.\nPull request: ​https://github.com/django/django/pull/10097\nMake URLValidator reject invalid characters in the username and password\nDescription\n\t \n\t\t(last modified by Tim Bell)\n\t \nSince #20003, core.validators.URLValidator accepts URLs with usernames and passwords. RFC 1738 section 3.1 requires \"Within the user and password field, any \":\", \"@\", or \"/\" must be encoded\"; however, those characters are currently accepted without being %-encoded. That allows certain invalid URLs to pass validation incorrectly. (The issue originates in Diego Perini's ​gist, from which the implementation in #20003 was derived.)\nAn example URL that should be invalid is http://foo/bar@example.com; furthermore, many of the test cases in tests/validators/invalid_urls.txt would be rendered valid under the current implementation by appending a query string of the form ?m=foo@example.com to them.\nI note Tim Graham's concern about adding complexity to the validation regex. However, I take the opposite position to Danilo Bargen about invalid URL edge cases: it's not fine if invalid URLs (even so-called \"edge cases\") are accepted when the regex could be fixed simply to reject them correctly. I also note that a URL of the form above was encountered in a production setting, so that this is a genuine use case, not merely an academic exercise.\nPull request: ​https://github.com/django/django/pull/10097\n</github_issue>\n\nCan you help me implement the necessary changes to the repository to fix the <github_issue>?\nI have already taken care of all changes to any of the test files described in the <github_issue>. This means you DON'T have to modify the testing logic or any of the tests in any way! Your task is to make changes to non-test files in the /testbed directory to ensure the <github_issue> is resolved.\n\nFollow these steps to resolve the issue:\n1. First, explore the codebase to locate and understand the code relevant to the <github_issue>. \n  - Use efficient search commands to identify key files and functions. \n  - You should err on the side of caution and look at various relevant files and build your understanding of \n    - how the code works\n    - what are the expected behaviors and edge cases\n    - what are the potential root causes for the given issue\n\n2. Assess whether you can reproduce the issue:\n   - Create a script at '/testbed/reproduce_issue.py' that demonstrates the error.\n   - Execute this script to confirm the error behavior.\n   - You should reproduce the issue before fixing it.\n   - Your reproduction script should also assert the expected behavior for the fixed code. \n\n3. Analyze the root cause:\n   - Identify the underlying problem based on your code exploration and reproduction results.\n   - Critically analyze different potential approaches to fix the issue. \n   - You NEED to explicitly reason about multiple approaches to fix the issue. Next, find the most elegant and effective solution among them considering the tradeoffs (correctness, generality, side effects, etc.).\n   - You would need to reason about execution paths, edge cases, and other potential issues. You should look at the unit tests to understand the expected behavior of the relevant code.\n\n4. Implement your solution:\n   - Make targeted changes to the necessary files following idiomatic code patterns once you determine the root cause.\n   - You should be thorough and methodical.\n\n5. Verify your solution:\n   - Rerun your reproduction script to confirm the error is fixed.\n   - If verification fails, iterate on your solution until successful. If you identify the reproduction script is buggy, adjust it as needed.\n\n6. Run unit tests:\n    - Find and run the relevant unit tests relevant to the performed fix.\n    - You should run the unit tests to ensure your solution is correct and does not cause any regressions.\n    - In cases where the unit tests are do not pass, you should consider whether the unit tests does not reflect the *new* expected behavior of the code. If so, you can test it by writing additional edge test cases.\n    - Use the existing test runner to run the unit tests you identify as relevant to the changes you made. For example:\n       - `python -m pytest -xvs sympy/physics/units/tests/test_dimensions_transcendental.py`\n       - `python -m pytest tests/test_domain_py.py::test_pymethod_options`\n       - `./tests/runtests.py constraints.tests.CheckConstraintTests -v 2`\n    - RUN ALL relevant unit tests to ensure your solution is correct and does not cause any regressions.\n\n7. Test edge cases:\n   - Identify potential edge cases that might challenge your solution.\n   - Create additional test cases in a separate file '/testbed/edge_case_tests.py'.\n   - Execute these tests to verify your solution's robustness.\n   - You should run multiple rounds of edge cases. When creating edge cases:\n      - Consider complex scenarios beyond the original issue description\n      - Test for regressions to ensure existing functionality remains intact\n\n8. Refine if necessary:\n   - If edge case testing reveals issues, refine your solution accordingly.\n   - Ensure your final implementation handles all identified scenarios correctly.\n   - Document any assumptions or limitations of your solution.\n\nA successful resolution means:\n- The specific error/issue described no longer occurs\n- Your changes maintain compatibility with existing functionality\n- Edge cases are properly handled\n\n\nAdditional recommendations:\n- You should be thorough, methodical, and prioritize quality over speed. Be comprehensive.\n- You should think carefully before making the tool call about what should be done. However, each step should only use one tool call. YOU SHOULD NOT USE TOOLS INSIDE YOUR THOUGHT PROCESS. YOU SHOULD PRIMARILY USE THINKING FOR IDENTIFYING THE ROOT CAUSE OF THE ISSUE, MAKING THE CHANGES, AND CREATING TEST CASES (REPRODUCTION OR EDGE CASES).\n- Each action you take is somewhat expensive. Wherever possible, combine multiple actions into a single action (e.g., combine multiple bash commands, use sed/grep for bulk operations). \n    - Your grep commands should identify both relevant files and line numbers so you can use the file_editor tool.\n    - Use grep with `-A -B -C` flags to quickly identify the relevant code blocks during your exploration.\n- When exploring the codebase, use targeted search patterns to minimize unnecessary operations.\n- When creating edge cases, you should look at the relevant existing tests to understand existing \"regression\" test cases. Ensure the fix doesn't break existing functionality."}, {"content": "I'll help you implement the necessary changes to fix the URLValidator issue. Let's start by exploring the codebase to understand the current implementation.\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_9034631080_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"cmd\": \"find /testbed -type f -name \\\"*.py\\\" | grep -E \\\"(validat|url)\\\" | head -20\"}", "name": "execute_bash"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "Let me break down this issue:\n\n1. The issue is with Django's URLValidator which currently accepts URLs with usernames and passwords\n2. According to RFC 1738 section 3.1, characters like \":\", \"@\", or \"/\" must be encoded within the user and password fields\n3. Currently, these characters are accepted without being %-encoded, which allows invalid URLs to pass validation\n4. Example of an invalid URL that should be rejected: `http://foo/bar@example.com`\n\nLet me start by exploring the codebase to understand the current implementation of URLValidator.\n\nI need to:\n1. Find the URLValidator implementation\n2. Understand how it currently handles usernames and passwords\n3. Create a reproduction script\n4. Fix the issue\n5. Test the fix"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Exit code: 0\nExecution output of [execute_bash]:\n[STDOUT]\n\n/testbed/django/urls/converters.py\n/testbed/django/urls/base.py\n/testbed/django/urls/exceptions.py\n/testbed/django/urls/resolvers.py\n/testbed/django/urls/__init__.py\n/testbed/django/urls/conf.py\n/testbed/django/urls/utils.py\n/testbed/django/core/checks/urls.py\n/testbed/django/core/validators.py\n/testbed/django/db/backends/oracle/validation.py\n/testbed/django/db/backends/base/validation.py\n/testbed/django/db/backends/mysql/validation.py\n/testbed/django/contrib/flatpages/urls.py\n/testbed/django/contrib/admindocs/urls.py\n/testbed/django/contrib/staticfiles/urls.py\n/testbed/django/contrib/admin/templatetags/admin_urls.py\n/testbed/django/contrib/postgres/validators.py\n/testbed/django/contrib/auth/migrations/0007_alter_validators_add_error_messages.py\n/testbed/django/contrib/auth/urls.py\n/testbed/django/contrib/auth/validators.py \n\n[STDERR]\n\n", "name": "execute_bash", "tool_call_id": "call_function_9034631080_1"}, {"content": "\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_3287586603_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\": \"view\", \"path\": \"/testbed/django/core/validators.py\"}", "name": "file_editor"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "Great! I can see there's a `/testbed/django/core/validators.py` file which likely contains the URLValidator. Let me examine this file first."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Execution output of [file_editor]:\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/usr/local/bin/file_editor\", line 719, in main\n    concise=args.concise,\n  File \"/usr/local/bin/file_editor\", line 168, in run\n    return self.view(path, view_range, concise=concise, python_only=python_only)\n  File \"/usr/local/bin/file_editor\", line 294, in view\n    lines_with_original_numbers = self._get_elided_lines(path)\n  File \"/usr/local/bin/file_editor\", line 404, in _get_elided_lines\n    and isinstance(node.body[0].value, ast.Constant)\nAttributeError: module 'ast' has no attribute 'Constant'\nERROR: Unhandled exception: module 'ast' has no attribute 'Constant'", "name": "file_editor", "tool_call_id": "call_function_3287586603_1"}, {"content": "\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_2530554662_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"cmd\": \"head -50 /testbed/django/core/validators.py\"}", "name": "execute_bash"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "There seems to be an issue with the file_editor. Let me try a different approach to view the file."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Exit code: 0\nExecution output of [execute_bash]:\n[STDOUT]\n\nimport ipaddress\nimport re\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom urllib.parse import urlsplit, urlunsplit\n\nfrom django.core.exceptions import ValidationError\nfrom django.utils.deconstruct import deconstructible\nfrom django.utils.functional import SimpleLazyObject\nfrom django.utils.ipv6 import is_valid_ipv6_address\nfrom django.utils.translation import gettext_lazy as _, ngettext_lazy\n\n# These values, if given to validate(), will trigger the self.required check.\nEMPTY_VALUES = (None, '', [], (), {})\n\n\ndef _lazy_re_compile(regex, flags=0):\n    \"\"\"Lazily compile a regex with flags.\"\"\"\n    def _compile():\n        # Compile the regex if it was not passed pre-compiled.\n        if isinstance(regex, str):\n            return re.compile(regex, flags)\n        else:\n            assert not flags, \"flags must be empty if regex is passed pre-compiled\"\n            return regex\n    return SimpleLazyObject(_compile)\n\n\n@deconstructible\nclass RegexValidator:\n    regex = ''\n    message = _('Enter a valid value.')\n    code = 'invalid'\n    inverse_match = False\n    flags = 0\n\n    def __init__(self, regex=None, message=None, code=None, inverse_match=None, flags=None):\n        if regex is not None:\n            self.regex = regex\n        if message is not None:\n            self.message = message\n        if code is not None:\n            self.code = code\n        if inverse_match is not None:\n            self.inverse_match = inverse_match\n        if flags is not None:\n            self.flags = flags\n        if self.flags and not isinstance(self.regex, str):\n            raise TypeError(\"If the flags are set, regex must be a regular expression string.\")\n\n        self.regex = _lazy_re_compile(self.regex, self.flags) \n\n[STDERR]\n\n", "name": "execute_bash", "tool_call_id": "call_function_2530554662_1"}, {"content": "\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_8050145155_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"cmd\": \"grep -n \\\"class URLValidator\\\" /testbed/django/core/validators.py\"}", "name": "execute_bash"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "reasoning_content": "Let me search for the URLValidator class specifically in this file."}, {"role": "tool", "content": "Exit code: 0\nExecution output of [execute_bash]:\n[STDOUT]\n\n74:class URLValidator(RegexValidator): \n\n[STDERR]\n\n", "name": "execute_bash", "tool_call_id": "call_function_8050145155_1"}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "file_editor", "description": "Custom editing tool for viewing, creating and editing files\n* State is persistent across command calls and discussions with the user\n* If `path` is a file, `view` displays the result of applying `cat -n`. If `path` is a directory, `view` lists non-hidden files and directories up to 2 levels deep\n* The `create` command cannot be used if the specified `path` already exists as a file\n* If a `command` generates a long output, it will be truncated and marked with `<response clipped>`\n* The `undo_edit` command will revert the last edit made to the file at `path`\n\nNotes for using the `str_replace` command:\n* The `old_str` parameter should match EXACTLY one or more consecutive lines from the original file. Be mindful of whitespaces!\n* If the `old_str` parameter is not unique in the file, the replacement will not be performed. Make sure to include enough context in `old_str` to make it unique\n* The `new_str` parameter should contain the edited lines that should replace the `old_str`\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"command": {"description": "The command to run. Allowed options are: `view`, `create`, `str_replace`, `insert`, `undo_edit`.", "enum": ["view", "create", "str_replace", "insert", "undo_edit"], "type": "string"}, "path": {"description": "Absolute path to file or directory, e.g. `/testbed/file.py` or `/testbed`.", "type": "string"}, "file_text": {"description": "Required for the `create` command, contains the content of the file to be created.", "type": "string"}, "old_str": {"description": "Required for the `str_replace` command, specifies the string in `path` to replace.", "type": "string"}, "new_str": {"description": "Optional for the `str_replace` command to specify the replacement string. Required for the `insert` command to specify the string to insert.", "type": "string"}, "insert_line": {"description": "Required for the `insert` command. The `new_str` will be inserted AFTER the line specified.", "type": "integer"}, "view_range": {"description": "Optional for the `view` command when `path` points to a file. Specifies the line range to view. E.g., [11, 12] shows lines 11 and 12. Indexing starts at 1. Use [start_line, -1] to show all lines from `start_line` to the end.", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}}, "concise": {"description": "Optional for the `view` command. If `True`, displays a concise skeletal view of the file. Very useful for localization tasks. Highly recommended for large files.", "type": "boolean"}}, "required": ["command", "path"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "execute_bash", "description": "\nDescription: Execute a bash command in the terminal.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) command (string, required): The bash command to execute. For example: `python my_script.py`\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"cmd": {"type": "string", "description": "The command (and optional arguments) to execute. For example: 'python my_script.py'"}}, "required": ["cmd"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search", "description": "\nDescription: Search for a term in either a directory or a single file.\n\nBehavior:\n* If `--path` points to a directory (default is `.`), we recursively search all non-hidden files and directories.\n* If `--path` points to a file, we run `grep -n` on that file to find line numbers containing the search term.\n* If more than 100 files match (directory search scenario), the tool will stop listing and inform you to narrow your search.\n* If no files are found that match your search term, the tool will inform you of that as well.\n\n**Parameters:**\n  1. **search_term** (`string`, required): The term to search for in files.\n  2. **path** (`string`, optional): The file or directory in which to search. If not provided, defaults to the current directory (i.e., `.`).\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_term": {"description": "The term to search for in files.", "type": "string"}, "path": {"description": "The file or directory to search in. Defaults to `.` if not specified.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["search_term"]}}}], "model": "minimax-m2.5"}, "response": {"id": "06450058a20eb3c5ef2611955f386a22", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "tool_calls", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "<think>\nLet me see the URLValidator class and its regex.\n</think>\n\n\n", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_function_2d2bsojh34v6_1", "function": {"arguments": "{\"cmd\": \"sed -n '74,200p' /testbed/django/core/validators.py\"}", "name": "execute_bash"}, "type": "function", "index": 0}], "name": "MiniMax AI", "audio_content": ""}}], "created": 1777716568, "model": "MiniMax-M2.5", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 57, "prompt_tokens": 4221, "total_tokens": 4278, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 15, "rejected_p